\noindent\emph{This vignette is the manuscript submitted to the \emph{Journal of Statistical
Software}, compiled with \pkg{baggingReserve} \Sexpr{packageVersion("baggingReserve")}, the version
submitted with it. The supplementary material, the replication scripts and the deposited
results accompany the submission.}

%% =============================================================================
\section{Introduction} \label{sec:intro}

A non-life insurer must hold reserves for the payments it still owes on claims that have already
occurred. At a valuation date $t$, these claims are of two kinds: claims already reported to
the insurer, open or closed (a closed claim can reopen), and claims incurred but not yet
reported (IBNR). Classical reserving methods such as the chain ladder
\citep{Mack:1993, England+Verrall:2002} work on run-off triangles, i.e., on payments aggregated
by accident year and development year; machine-learning extensions of the aggregate models
exist as well \citep{Gabrielli+Richman+Wuthrich:2020}. Aggregation discards the information
carried by the individual claim: its type, characteristics of the insured, reporting time and
payment history. Individual (micro-level) reserving methods keep it; see
\citet{Wuthrich:2018} for a machine-learning formulation on which the method implemented here
builds. Tree-based individual methods include the censored regression trees of
\citet{LopezMilhaudTherond2016, LopezEtAl2019}, the nonparametric approach of
\citet{BaudryRobert2019} and the gradient boosting of \citet{Duval+Pigeon:2019};
\citet{DelongLindholmWuthrich2022} use individual claims data in a collective reserving model,
and \citet{Hiabu+Hofman+Pittarello:2023} forecast the numbers of IBNR claims from individual
reporting data with feature-dependent development factors.

In what we call the source article, \citet{Janousek+Pesta:2025} predict the outstanding
payments claim by claim. For each development year, one regression tree predicts a reported
claim's payment in the next year from its static features and its payment history; the trees
are applied recursively to complete every reported claim's payment history; bootstrap
aggregating (bagging, \citealp{Breiman:1996}) yields replicate reserve predictions and an
out-of-bag (OOB) diagnostic; and payments of IBNR claims are estimated from chain-ladder
projections of the reporting counts and the average payment by development year. The method was
originally implemented in a script around \pkg{rpart} \citep{rpart} and \pkg{ipred}
\citep{ipred}.

This paper presents \pkg{baggingReserve}, an \proglang{R} package for claim-level reserving and
for its evaluation against realised payments. It makes three contributions. First, a \emph{data contract}: the constructor
\fct{claims\_panel} takes a table of claims and a table of payment transactions with
user-specified column names, enforces the valuation date on both, distinguishes observed zeros
from future cells, keeps recoveries and claims without payments, and rejects input it cannot
interpret. Second, a \emph{recursion engine} shared by the single-tree fit, the bagged ensemble
and user-supplied learners, with a checked learner interface and two completion modes, plugged-in
means and simulated payments; its bagging draws random numbers from streams allocated to
replicates and development years, so that the results do not depend on the number of worker
processes, and each prediction states what its columns and their quantiles mean.
Third, \emph{back-testing}: \fct{backtest} compares a prediction with the payments that
followed, by calendar year and separately for reported and IBNR claims, and scores a reference
method such as the chain ladder against the same truth; valuing a portfolio at an earlier date
turns any claims history into such a test.

The method of the source article is the default estimator, a documented, reproducible
claim-level baseline against which other learners and the chain ladder can be measured; it is
not more accurate than the chain ladder on most portfolios studied here
(Section~\ref{sec:evaluation}), and it has not been validated against the source article's
numbers, whose portfolio is not available. The package is best understood as a harness for
finding out whether claim-level information helps on one's own data.

\begin{table}[t!]
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{@{}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{2.3cm}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{1.9cm}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{2.6cm}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{2.2cm}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{1.6cm}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{3.6cm}@{}}
\toprule
Software & Input & Target & IBNR claims & Open vs.\ closed claims & Beyond the point prediction \\
\midrule
\pkg{ChainLadder}, \pkg{chainladder} & triangle & aggregate payments & in the aggregate & not separated & Mack standard errors, bootstrap, further aggregate models \\
\pkg{DCL} & triangles of counts and payments & aggregate payments, RBNS and IBNR & counts $\times$ estimated severity & not separated & bootstrap predictive distribution \\
\pkg{ReSurv} & individual claims & numbers of IBNR claims & counts only & -- & none \\
\pkg{hirem} & individual claims & payments of open reported claims & not modelled & settlement layer & simulated paths \\
\midrule
\pkg{baggingReserve} & claims + payments & payments per claim and development year & counts $\times$ pooled mean payment & not modelled (\mbox{\supp{5}}) & replicate quantiles (resampling variation only); OOB diagnostic; back-tests \\
