\end{tabular}
\caption{Software related to \pkg{baggingReserve}. Only the Mack and bootstrap outputs of the
chain-ladder packages, the bootstrap of \pkg{DCL} and the simulated paths of \pkg{hirem} quantify reserve uncertainty
(Section~\ref{sec:bagging} explains the replicate quantiles and out-of-bag diagnostic of
\pkg{baggingReserve}).}
\label{tab:software}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:software} compares the package with related software. \pkg{ChainLadder}
\citep{ChainLadder} in \proglang{R} and \pkg{chainladder} \citep{chainladder-python} in
\proglang{Python} implement aggregate reserving on triangles, from the chain ladder with Mack's
standard errors to bootstrap and further models; \pkg{baggingReserve} uses only the standard
chain ladder, as a comparator and for the count projection, and is no substitute for these
packages in aggregate reserving. The double chain ladder of \citet{MartinezMiranda+Nielsen+Verrall:2012},
implemented in \pkg{DCL} \citep{DCL}, adds the triangle of reported claim counts to the paid
triangle, estimates a reporting delay, a payment delay and a mean payment per claim, and so
separates the reserve into reported but not settled (RBNS) and IBNR claims, with a bootstrap for
its predictive distribution. Its IBNR part has the structure of the IBNR component here,
projected counts times a payment per claim, and it is the natural stronger aggregate comparator;
this paper does not use it (Section~\ref{sec:comparators}). \pkg{ReSurv} \citep{ReSurv} implements the IBNR count models of
\citet{Hiabu+Hofman+Pittarello:2023} and does not model payments; its counts could replace the
chain-ladder count projection of the IBNR component, which the package does not automate.
\pkg{hirem} \citep{Crevecoeur+Robben+Antonio:2022, hirem} fits sequential layers (settlement,
payment occurrence, payment size) to one row per claim and development year counted from
reporting and simulates the payment paths of the claims open at the valuation date; claims not yet
reported are outside the model and a closed claim pays nothing further. It thus models the payment
process of open claims more richly, whereas \pkg{baggingReserve} completes every reported claim,
open or closed, from the two raw tables with one regression per development year, allows payments
of either sign and adds an IBNR component, but cannot allocate the reported-claim reserve reliably
between open and closed claims; on that target \pkg{hirem} is the more accurate of the two
(Section~\ref{sec:evaluation}). Generic ensemble packages \citep{ipred, randomForest,
Wright+Ziegler:2017} supply learners but none of the reserving structure, and \pkg{SynthETIC}
\citep{Avanzi+Taylor+Wang+Wong:2021} and \pkg{SPLICE} \citep{Avanzi+Taylor+Wang:2023} simulate
the individual claims data such methods are tested on.

Section~\ref{sec:method} states the method as implemented and Section~\ref{sec:design}
describes the package. Section~\ref{sec:illustration} illustrates it on a toy example, on the
shipped portfolio, at earlier valuation dates and on a motor third-party liability dataset,
summarises the evaluation of the method, and describes how to reproduce the results.
Section~\ref{sec:summary} concludes. The supplementary material, whose sections are cited as S1 to
S9, holds the detailed evaluation:
the generator of the shipped data, a second portfolio with another data layout, further
portfolios, the open/closed allocation, a changing business mix, studies of simulated
completion, other learners and \pkg{hirem}, the motor case in detail, and the leaf size of the
bootstrap trees.

%% =============================================================================
\section{Reserving with regression trees and bagging} \label{sec:method}

\subsection{Notation and data}

Time is discrete, in years. A claim $r$ has an accident year $a_r$, is reported with a delay of
$d_r \in \{0, 1, \dots\}$ years, and generates incremental payments $X_{r,0}, X_{r,1}, \dots$,
where $X_{r,l}$ is the total paid in development year $l$, i.e., in calendar year $a_r + l$;
payments can be negative (recoveries) and are zero before the claim is reported. A maximum
development year $L$ is fixed: payments beyond $L$ are outside the model. The valuation date is
the end of year $t$. The insurer then knows every claim with $a_r + d_r \le t$ (the reported
claims), and for each of them the payments $X_{r,l}$ with $a_r + l \le t$; the cells with
$a_r + l > t$ represent future payments. Summing over the claims of an accident year $i$ gives
the familiar run-off triangle, observed above the diagonal $i + l = t$.

The static features of a claim (in the source article: type, age of the insured, weekday of
the accident) and its reporting delay form the covariates. Let $\mathbf{x}_{r,l} = (\text{features}_r,
d_r, X_{r,0}, \dots, X_{r,l})$ denote the information about claim $r$ up to development year $l$.
The model of the source article assumes that $X_{r,l+1}$ given $\mathbf{x}_{r,l}$ has a conditional
distribution $p_l(\mathbf{x}_{r,l})$ that does not depend on the accident year; the regression
tree estimates the conditional mean of that distribution. With $\mathcal{F}_t$ the information
available at the valuation date, the target is the reserve
$\mathrm{E}[\sum_r \sum_{l \le L,\; a_r + l > t} X_{r,l} \mid \mathcal{F}_t]$, the sum running
over reported and unreported claims; for an unreported claim, $a_r + d_r > t$, the summands are its
whole payments $X_{r,l}$ with $l \ge d_r$, which Section~\ref{sec:ibnr} estimates in the aggregate. For a reported claim more than one year from the valuation
date, the method plugs the predicted mean of each future payment into the history used by the
next tree (Section~\ref{sec:trees}). Because the trees are nonlinear functions of the history,
the mean of a tree's prediction is in general not the tree's prediction at the mean history, so
this recursion approximates the conditional expectation and does not equal it;
Section~\ref{sec:learners} describes the simulated alternative.

\subsection{One tree per development year and recursive completion} \label{sec:trees}

For every development year $l = 0, \dots, L - 1$, a regression tree $T_l$ is fitted with
response $X_{r,l+1}$ and covariates $\mathbf{x}_{r,l}$ on the training set
\begin{equation}
  S_l = \{ r : d_r \le l \text{ and } a_r + l + 1 \le t \},
\end{equation}
the claims that were reported by development year $l$ and whose payment in development year
$l + 1$ is observed at the valuation date. Payments before reporting are structurally zero and
are neither predicted nor used as responses: a claim enters the training set of development
year $l$ only from its reporting year on. The trees are \pkg{rpart} analysis-of-variance
(ANOVA) trees \citep{rpart, Breiman+Friedman+Olshen+Stone:1984}, grown with complexity
parameter zero and pruned to the subtree with the smallest 10-fold cross-validated error (the
simplest one on ties), as in the source article, not by \pkg{rpart}'s customary
one-standard-error rule, which prunes more.

Prediction proceeds year by year. The tree $T_l$ predicts $X_{r,l+1}$ for the claims with
$a_r + l + 1 > t$; its payment-history covariates $X_{r,0}, \dots, X_{r,l}$ are observed where
$a_r + k \le t$ and are the predictions of earlier trees where $a_r + k > t$. Every reported
claim is completed, including claims recorded as closed, because a claim can reopen; the trees
therefore do not distinguish open from closed claims, and naming the status at the valuation
date as a feature does not fix this (\supp{5}).
Figure~\ref{fig:schematic} shows the pattern of observed, predicted and unavailable cells.

<<schematic, echo=FALSE, fig.width=6.5, fig.height=2.9, out.width="0.46\\textwidth", fig.cap="Cells of the run-off triangle for a portfolio with $t = 10$ and $L = 9$ (smaller than the shipped one, for legibility): rows represent accident years (each standing for all claims of that year) and columns represent development years. Dark: observed at the valuation date. Light: predicted recursively, development year by development year from left to right. Hatched: training cells for the tree indexed by $l = 2$, covariates (columns 0--2, hatched one way) and response (column 3, hatched the other way) of the claims reported by development year 2 with accident year $\\le 7$.">>=
op <- par(mar = c(3, 3.5, 0.5, 0.5), mgp = c(2, 0.6, 0))
plot(NA, xlim = c(0, 10), ylim = c(10, 0), xlab = "development year l", ylab = "accident year",
     axes = FALSE, asp = 1)
axis(1, at = 0:9 + 0.5, labels = 0:9, tick = FALSE); axis(2, at = 1:10 - 0.5, labels = 1:10, tick = FALSE, las = 1)
for (a in 1:10) for (l in 0:9) {
  obs <- a + l <= 10
  rect(l, a - 1, l + 1, a, col = if (obs) "grey45" else "grey90", border = "white")
}
for (a in 1:7) rect(0, a - 1, 3, a, density = 18, angle = 45, border = NA, col = "black")
for (a in 1:7) rect(3, a - 1, 4, a, density = 18, angle = -45, border = NA, col = "black")
par(op)
@

\subsection{Bagging, replicates and the out-of-bag score} \label{sec:bagging}

Bagging replaces each tree $T_l$ by an ensemble $T_{l,1}, \dots, T_{l,B}$. Each tree $T_{l,b}$
is grown on a bootstrap sample (drawn with replacement, duplicates kept) of $S_l$, drawn
independently for each development year rather than once per claim. Unlike the
single trees of Section~\ref{sec:trees}, the bootstrap trees are grown with complexity
parameter zero and left unpruned, without cross-validation (pruning and cross-validation are
configurable through arguments), as in the source article's code. The source article
and the package define \emph{replicate} $b$ as the completion of all payment histories with the
trees $T_{0,b}, \dots, T_{L-1,b}$, one per development year, applied recursively as in
Section~\ref{sec:trees}. Writing $F$ for the completion map,
\begin{equation}
  R_b = F(T_{0,b}, \dots, T_{L-1,b}), \qquad \bar R = \frac{1}{B} \sum_{b=1}^B R_b,
\end{equation}
the bagged reserve prediction is the mean $\bar R$ of the completed replicates. Because the
recursion is nonlinear, this is not the same as completing the histories once with tree
predictions averaged within each development year, which the package does not implement. The empirical quantiles of $R_1, \dots,
R_B$, the \emph{replicate quantiles}, describe only the variation, under bootstrap resampling,
of the reserve predicted by one set of unpruned bootstrap trees: the numbers of unreported
claims are projected once and held fixed across replicates (Section~\ref{sec:ibnr}), no
payment noise is simulated, and because the samples are drawn per development year they do not
reproduce how the errors of successive trees are linked through the same claims. The replicate
quantiles are therefore neither prediction intervals for future payments nor confidence
intervals for the bagged mean, a realised value inside their range is no coverage statement, and
the package delivers no predictive distribution of the reserve (``resampling variation'' below).

For the out-of-bag diagnostic of development year $l$, let $\mathcal{B}_l(r)$ be the set of
replicates whose bootstrap sample of $S_l$ did not contain claim $r$, and let
$\mathrm{OOB}_l(r) = |\mathcal{B}_l(r)|^{-1} \sum_{b \in \mathcal{B}_l(r)} T_{l,b}(\mathbf{x}_{r,l})$
denote the average prediction of those trees, computed from the claim's \emph{observed}
history. With $S_l^\ast = \{ r \in S_l : |\mathcal{B}_l(r)| > 0 \}$ denoting the set of covered
claims and $y_r = X_{r,l+1}$,
\begin{equation}
  \mathrm{OOB\text{-}error}_l = \frac{1}{|S_l^\ast|} \sum_{r \in S_l^\ast} (y_r - \mathrm{OOB}_l(r))^2,
  \qquad
  \mathrm{OOB\text{-}score}_l = 1 - \frac{\sum_{r \in S_l^\ast} (y_r - \mathrm{OOB}_l(r))^2}
                                        {\sum_{r \in S_l} (y_r - \bar y_l)^2},
  \label{eq:oob}
\end{equation}
where the denominator runs over all training claims of development year $l$ and $\bar y_l$ is
their mean response. With complete coverage the score is interpreted like $R^2$: one for a
perfect fit, negative when the ensemble is worse than predicting the mean. A claim is left out
of a bootstrap sample of $n_l$ claims with probability $(1 - 1/n_l)^{n_l} \approx e^{-1}$, so it
is covered with probability about $1 - (1 - e^{-1})^B$: coverage is essentially complete for
$B$ in the hundreds but not for very small $B$, and then numerator and denominator run over
different sets and the score is biased upwards. The package therefore reports the coverage and
also the ordinary coefficient of determination over the covered claims alone
(\code{oob\_score\_covered}). The score assesses the one-year regressions given observed
histories; it does not validate the recursive multi-year forecast or the IBNR component.

\subsection{Claims incurred but not reported} \label{sec:ibnr}

Let $N_{i,d}$ be the number of claims of accident year $i$ reported with delay $d$; the
triangle of these counts is observed for $i + d \le t$. Theorem~1 of the source article
expresses the expected payments of the unreported claims of accident year $i$ as the product of
the expected number of claims and the expected payments per claim, when the payments of a claim
reported after $t$ are independent of the information at $t$; the unreported claims have no
recorded features, delay or history, so the trees cannot be applied to them. The package
implements the source article's estimator of this product. It completes the count triangle by
a chain-ladder projection, which is justified when claims arrive as a homogeneous marked Poisson
process whose reporting-delay distribution does not depend on the accident year
\citep[Section~6.1]{Verrall+Wuthrich:2016}; $\widehat U_{i,l} = \sum_{d \le l} \widehat
N_{i,d}\,\mathbf{1}(i + d > t)$ is the projected number of claims of accident year $i$ reported
after the valuation date with delay at most $l$. The payment per such claim in development year
$l$ is estimated by the pooled mean payment of the reported claims in that development year,
\begin{equation}
  \hat\mu_l = \frac{\sum_i \widehat R_{i,l}}{\sum_i \sum_{d \le l,\; i + d \le t} N_{i,d}},
  \label{eq:mu}
\end{equation}
where, for a fixed replicate, $\widehat R_{i,l}$ denotes the sum of the observed and predicted
payments of the reported claims of accident year $i$ in development year $l$, and the
denominator counts the reported claims of all accident years with delay at most $l$ (observed
counts only). Because the numerator contains the replicate's predicted payments of the reported
claims, an error of the reported-claim component propagates into the IBNR component. The IBNR payment in cell $(i, l)$ is $\widehat U_{i,l}\,\hat\mu_l$; it is zero in
observed cells because every claim of accident year $i$ reported after $t$ has delay $d > t -
i$. The projected counts are the same in every replicate, so the replicate spread of the IBNR
component comes from $\hat\mu_l$ alone and contains no count uncertainty.

The estimator transfers the pooled payment behaviour of the reported claims to the
late-reported claims. It requires that, in development year $l$, a claim of accident year $i$
reported after $t$, with delay $t - i < d \le l$, has the same expected payment as the pooled
mean of the reported claims with delay at most $l$. Because $\hat\mu_l$ pools all accident
years, this needs both that, given $l$, the expected payment does not depend on the reporting
delay among claims with $d \le l$, and that it does not depend on the accident year: a trend in
the payments across accident years, such as the inflation of the generator of
Section~\ref{sec:portfolio}, also biases $\hat\mu_l$ for the recent accident years that carry most
IBNR claims. A
payment pattern that shifts with the delay, $g(l - d)$ say, violates this condition even when
every claim has the same payment pattern after reporting: with delays $0$ and $1$ and $n_0$ and
$n_1$ reported claims, respectively, the pooled mean differs from the expected payment of a
late-reported claim with delay $1$ by $n_0\,[g(l) - g(l-1)]/(n_0 + n_1)$, so a decreasing
pattern makes the estimator underpredict the payments of late-reported claims. A different
business mix among early- and late-reported claims also violates the condition, in either
direction. The pooling assumption is the source article's working approximation; its
suitability should be questioned when a portfolio's late-reported claims differ in kind
(\supp{3}). The default count projection is the standard chain ladder on cumulative counts
without rounding; the source article's incremental ratios with rounding are an option
(Section~\ref{sec:comparators}).

%% =============================================================================
\section{Package design} \label{sec:design}

The package is organised along the workflow construct $\to$ fit $\to$ predict $\to$ aggregate
$\to$ diagnose $\to$ back-test. Table~\ref{tab:api} lists the objects and their methods.

\begin{table}[t!]
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{ll>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{7.6cm}}
\toprule
Function & Returns & Methods and functions applying to the result \\
\midrule
\fct{claims\_panel} & \class{claims\_panel} & \code{print}, \code{summary}, \code{as.data.frame}, \fct{as\_triangle}, \fct{claim\_counts} \\
\fct{reserve\_tree} & \class{reserve\_tree} & \code{print}, \code{summary}, \code{predict}, \code{plot} \\
\fct{bag\_reserve} & \class{bag\_reserve} & \code{print}, \code{summary}, \code{predict}, \code{plot}, \fct{oob\_score} \\
\code{predict()} on a fit & \class{reserve\_prediction} & \code{print}, \code{summary}, \code{plot}, \code{as.data.frame}, \fct{as\_triangle}, \fct{calendar\_year\_payments}, \fct{claim\_reserves} \\
\fct{ibnr\_reserve} & \class{reserve\_prediction} & adds the IBNR component to a prediction \\
\fct{chain\_ladder} & \class{chain\_ladder} & \code{print}, \fct{as\_triangle}, \fct{calendar\_year\_payments} \\
\fct{backtest} & \class{reserve\_backtest} & \code{print}, \code{as.data.frame} \\
\fct{two\_part\_learner} & list & the argument \code{learner} of \fct{reserve\_tree} and \fct{bag\_reserve} (Section~\ref{sec:learners}) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Exported functions, the classes of their return values and the methods and functions that apply to them. Objects of these classes are S3 lists; \fct{as\_triangle}, \fct{calendar\_year\_payments} and \fct{oob\_score} are generics.}
\label{tab:api}
\end{table}

\subsection{The data contract}

\fct{claims\_panel} takes two data frames, one with a row per claim and the other with a row
per payment transaction, the valuation date \code{t}, the horizon \code{L} and the names of the
columns holding the identifier (\code{id}), the accident year (\code{acc\_year}), the reporting
year or delay (\code{rep\_year}, \code{rep\_delay}), the payment year (\code{event\_year}) and
the amount (\code{paid}), with the defaults of the shipped data (Table~\ref{tab:defaults} lists
the other arguments). The argument \code{id} may name a different identifier column for each table. The constructor returns a claims table (identifier, accident year, reporting delay and
features), a payment matrix with one row per reported claim and columns \code{X0}, \ldots,
\code{XL}, the observation mask and construction diagnostics. Claims are retained if reported
by \code{t}, and payments are summed by claim and development year; times must be integers
(dates are converted by the user, as in Section~\ref{sec:mtpl}), on any regular grid: years
here, but quarters or months numbered consecutively work alike. Observed cells without
payments contain \code{0}, cells after the valuation date \code{NA}; recoveries and claims
without transactions are retained. The constructor rejects duplicated or missing identifiers,
payments for claims absent from the claims table or for claims not reported by \code{t},
payments dated before the accident or the reporting year, reporting delays or observed payments
beyond \code{L} unless \code{beyond\_L = "drop"} sets them aside (for a long history), and payments
dated after \code{t} unless \code{future\_payments = "drop"} does so; the numbers and amounts set
aside are recorded, and the identifiers of the claims set aside travel with the predictions to
\fct{backtest}. Features are never selected automatically, and
only static features are supported: information that changes over the life of a claim, such as
its settlement status or case reserve at the end of each development year, cannot enter the
panel. This is the main limitation of the design and the reason why the package cannot allocate
the reserve between open and closed claims. Character columns become factors with sorted levels;
missing feature values are rejected unless \code{na\_features = "surrogate"} lets \pkg{rpart}'s
surrogate splits handle them. The reporting delay is always a covariate, and \code{L} is never
inferred from the data. \fct{as\_triangle} sums the panel into the paid triangle (incremental or
cumulative) or the triangle of reporting counts, with \code{NA} in the cells after the valuation
date.

\subsection{Fitting and prediction} \label{sec:fitting}

\fct{reserve\_tree} fits the $L$ pruned trees of Section~\ref{sec:trees}; \fct{bag\_reserve}
fits the $B \times L$ bootstrap trees of Section~\ref{sec:bagging}. In the package's arguments,
printed output and tables, the development-year index $l$ of a tree is called its
\code{period}. Both functions use the same internal recursion engine. For each development
year, the eligibility conditions determine the training rows and the model frame; the frame's
column order (features, reporting delay, \code{X0}, \ldots, \code{Xl}, response) is fixed, so
that \pkg{rpart}'s tie-breaking between equally good splits is reproducible; and the recursive
completion overwrites only the future cells of the current step, using the completed cells of
the earlier steps. \Sexpr{hyper_txt}

Calling \fct{predict} on either fit returns a \class{reserve\_prediction}: the observed
incremental triangle, a table of the future cells (accident year, development year, calendar
year), and a matrix with one row per future cell and one column per replicate of the predicted
payments of the reported claims (one column for a single-tree fit), and the completed claim-level
payment matrix, from which \fct{claim\_reserves} returns the reserve of every reported claim. \fct{calendar\_year\_payments} sums the cells along the diagonals into
payments per future calendar year plus their total through development year $L$; \fct{summary}
gives the mean, standard deviation and empirical quantiles of these across the replicates, with
the quantile type as an argument. The default is \code{type = 7}, \proglang{R}'s default; the
source article used order statistics, which \code{type = 1} reproduces.

\subsection{Other learners and simulated completion} \label{sec:learners}

Both fitting functions accept an argument \code{learner}, which replaces the \pkg{rpart} trees in
every development year while the eligibility rules, the bootstrap, the recursion, the out-of-bag
score and the random-number streams stay the package's. A learner is a list of functions:
\code{fit(formula, data)} returns a model for one development year $l$; \code{formula} is
\code{X}$(l+1)$\code{ ~ .} and \code{data} the model frame of Section~\ref{sec:fitting}, with the
features, \code{rep\_delay} and the payment history \code{X0}, \ldots, \code{X}$l$ as columns, one
row per training claim (for \fct{bag\_reserve}, per bootstrap draw). \code{predict(model, newdata)}
returns one number per row of \code{newdata}, which has the same columns without the response.
An optional \code{simulate(model, newdata)} returns one simulated payment per row. Every prediction
of a learner, for the completion and the out-of-bag votes alike, receives this frame and is
checked: a numeric vector with one finite value per row, else an error naming the stage and the
development year. \fct{two\_part\_learner} builds a learner from one model for whether a claim pays
in the development year, fitted to a 0/1 response, and one for the amount of a non-zero payment,
and checks the output of each, probabilities in $[0, 1]$ included, before combining them; it
predicts $p \cdot s$ and simulates by drawing the payment indicator, the amount being the
predicted $s$ unless the size model has a \code{simulate()} of its own. Here both parts
are regression trees, on the claims of type 3 of the shipped portfolio:
<<learner-example>>=
library("baggingReserve")
data("synthetic_claims", "synthetic_payments", package = "baggingReserve")
cl3 <- synthetic_claims[synthetic_claims$Type == "3", ]
pay3 <- synthetic_payments[synthetic_payments$Id %in% cl3$Id, ]
panel3 <- claims_panel(cl3, pay3, t = 12, L = 11,
  features = c("Age", "AccWeekday"))
tree_learner <- function(...) list(
  fit = function(formula, data) rpart::rpart(formula, data = data,
    method = "anova", control = rpart::rpart.control(...)),
  predict = function(model, newdata) unname(predict(model, newdata)))
tp <- two_part_learner(occurrence = tree_learner(cp = 0.001, minbucket = 50),
  size = tree_learner(cp = 0.001, minbucket = 50))
fit_tp <- reserve_tree(panel3, seed = 1, learner = tp)
pred_tp <- predict(fit_tp, completion = "draw", R = 50, seed = 1)
cy_tp <- calendar_year_payments(pred_tp, "reported")
round(c(mean = mean(cy_tp["total", ]),
  quantile(cy_tp["total", ], c(0.025, 0.975))))
@
Instead of plugging the predicted mean of each development year into the next,
\code{completion = "draw"} simulates the payments: for the default trees each one is drawn from
the training responses of the claim's leaf, for a user-supplied learner from its
\code{simulate()}. The $R$ columns of the result are simulated completions of the fitted model,
so their spread is process variability given that model, without estimation variability.
\code{bag\_reserve(completion = "draw")} simulates one completion per bootstrap replicate from that
replicate's trees: the spread then adds process variability to the resampling variation of one
set of unpruned bootstrap trees (still not a predictive distribution; Section~\ref{sec:bagging}). For a bagged fit the completion is chosen when fitting;
\fct{predict} refuses a different one, and a fit with simulated completion predicts its own
portfolio only; \fct{print}, \fct{summary}, \fct{plot} and \fct{backtest} describe the sources of
variability of every prediction. The plug-in default reproduces the
source article, but it is not a consistent estimator of the multi-year reserve when the occurrence
of a payment depends on the payment history: in the correctly specified simulation of
Section~\ref{sec:well-specified} its bias for the total does not vanish as the portfolio grows,
while simulated completion removes most of it. For totals beyond next year we therefore recommend
\code{completion = "draw"}, and \fct{print} of a plug-in prediction says so. Neither completion
is the more accurate on every portfolio of Section~\ref{sec:illustration} (\supp{7}), and the
calibration of the simulated spread is not examined: a draw resamples the training responses of
one leaf, few in the late development years. \supp{7} applies
one- and two-part gradient boosting learners built the same way.
<<draw-claims, echo=FALSE>>=
# the sentence above: simulation helps the rolling back-tests on average, not the shipped portfolio at t = 12
if (have_rev && all(file.exists(file.path(rev_dir, c("bagging_draw.csv", "rolling_backtests.csv"))))) local({
  bd <- read.csv(file.path(rev_dir, "bagging_draw.csv")); bd <- bd[bd$component == "total", ]
  e <- setNames(bd$total_mean / bd$total_truth - 1, bd$completion)
  rl0 <- read.csv(file.path(rev_dir, "rolling_backtests.csv"))
  stopifnot(abs(e[["draw"]]) > abs(e[["mean"]]), mean(abs(rl0$draw_total_rel)) < mean(abs(rl0$bag_total_rel)))
})
@

\subsection{The IBNR component and the comparators} \label{sec:comparators}

\fct{ibnr\_reserve} adds to a prediction a second matrix of the same shape with the IBNR payments
of Section~\ref{sec:ibnr} and keeps the projected counts, the denominator of
Equation~\eqref{eq:mu} and the per-replicate $\hat\mu_l$ for inspection; counts that disagree
with the triangle stored in the prediction (another portfolio, cumulative counts) are an error. \fct{chain\_ladder}
projects a triangle with \code{NA} in the unobserved cells by the standard chain ladder:
volume-weighted development factors from the cumulative triangle over the accident years
observed in both columns, projection from the latest observed value, no tail factor, point
predictions only. The package's tests check its factors, completed triangle and outstanding
amounts against \code{ChainLadder::MackChainLadder()} with unit weights. Setting the argument
\code{projection} to \code{"incremental\_ratio"} applies ratios of incremental column sums to
the incremental cells instead, which is what the source article's code did for its chain-ladder
benchmark and for the count projection; \fct{ibnr\_reserve} provides the same option together
with \code{round = TRUE} for the source article's rounding of the projected counts.

As a comparator, this chain ladder is deliberately the plainest aggregate method: the paid
triangle alone, no tail factor, no incurred or count information. It has the same target as the
claim-level method, the payments through development year $L$. The comparisons in this paper are
with this standard chain ladder, not with the best aggregate method; a stronger one, from
\pkg{ChainLadder} or the double chain ladder of \pkg{DCL} say, can be scored against the same
truth through the argument \code{reference} of \fct{backtest}, which takes the prediction of any
method as payments by future calendar year.

\subsection{Back-testing} \label{sec:backtest}

<<backtest-contract, echo=FALSE>>=
bt_args_known <- c("pred", "claims", "payments", "id", "acc_year", "event_year", "paid", "probs", "rep_year", "type",
                   "reference", "reference_component", "observed_to")
bt_args_new <- setdiff(names(formals(baggingReserve::backtest)), bt_args_known)
if (length(bt_args_new))   # a newer package version changed the interface: the paragraph below must be revised
  stop("backtest() has arguments this section does not describe: ", paste(bt_args_new, collapse = ", "))
@
\fct{backtest} takes a prediction and the claims and payments tables of the same portfolio,
including the claims reported after the valuation date, and builds the realised payments of
every future cell through development year $L$, split into claims reported by $t$ and claims
reported later (the IBNR truth); payments beyond $L$ are reported as the excluded tail. For each
component it returns, per future calendar year and in total, the realised and predicted
payments, their difference and relative error (the difference divided by the realised amount, so
that its sign reverses in a calendar year of net recoveries), and the replicate range with
whether the truth lies inside it (descriptive only; Section~\ref{sec:bagging}). With \code{reference}, a
\class{chain\_ladder} object or a named vector of payments by calendar year, another method is
scored against the truth of the population it predicts: all claims by default, which requires
the prediction's IBNR component, or the reported claims (\code{reference\_component =
"reported"}). A \class{chain\_ladder} reference must have been fitted on the same panel (accident
years, development years, valuation date and observed payments are checked). The truth tables
must describe the whole portfolio: the function rejects claims absent from the prediction that
were reported by $t$, as a selection by the reported claims' identifiers would produce, and
predicted claims whose accident or reporting year contradicts the prediction, which stores
both; it refuses a payment before its claim's reporting year, as the constructor does, and counts
the claims set aside by \code{beyond\_L = "drop"} in the excluded tail, not as missing. Its column
names default to those given to \fct{claims\_panel}, and it takes the reporting year by the
constructor's rule, from the reporting delay when the panel was built from one; without a
reporting year these checks are skipped, with a warning. A future cell
without transactions counts as a realised zero; when the extract ends earlier,
\code{observed\_to} names the last fully observed calendar year, and later cells are left
unscored (\code{NA}) and out of the totals. With the reporting year, the result also compares the
projected with the realised numbers of IBNR claims by accident year (component \code{counts}),
which separates the count error of the IBNR component from the error of its mean payment.

\subsection{Reproducibility and parallel execution} \label{sec:storage}

<<rng-probe, echo=FALSE>>=
# Does reserve_tree(seed =) fix the generator kind? Fit a tiny panel under another session kind
# and read the kind the fit records (the text below depends on the installed version).
rng_tree_kind <- local({
  old <- RNGkind(); on.exit(RNGkind(old[1], old[2], old[3]))
  RNGkind("L'Ecuyer-CMRG")
  cl <- data.frame(Id = 1:40, AccYear = rep(1:4, each = 10), RepYear = rep(1:4, each = 10))
  py <- data.frame(Id = rep(1:40, 2), EventYear = c(cl$AccYear, pmin(cl$AccYear + 1, 4)), Paid = 1:80)
  py <- py[!duplicated(py[, 1:2]), ]
  f <- tryCatch(suppressWarnings(reserve_tree(claims_panel(cl, py, t = 4, L = 2), seed = 1)), error = function(e) NULL)
  if (is.null(f) || is.null(f$rng$kind)) NA_character_ else f$rng$kind[1]
})
rng_tree_txt <- if (is.na(rng_tree_kind)) "\\textbf{[the generator kind of \\fct{reserve\\_tree} could not be determined]}" else
  if (rng_tree_kind == "L'Ecuyer-CMRG") paste("\\fct{reserve\\_tree} draws random numbers only for the cross-validation folds of pruning. With \\code{seed},",
    "it calls \\code{set.seed(seed)} under the caller's generator kind and restores the caller's state on exit, so its folds, and hence the pruned",
    "trees, are reproducible only under the same kind; the single-tree fits of this paper use \\proglang{R}'s default kind.") else
  sprintf("\\fct{reserve\\_tree} draws random numbers only for the cross-validation folds; with \\code{seed}, it uses the generator kind \\code{\"%s\"} whatever the caller's kind, so the pruned trees do not depend on the session's generator.", rng_tree_kind)
@
\fct{bag\_reserve} processes the replicates without retaining their trees by default: a worker
fits the $L$ trees of a replicate on its bootstrap samples, records the out-of-bag predictions
(``votes''), completes the panel, keeps the aggregated future cells and discards the trees, unless
\code{keep\_trees = TRUE}; only with the trees (and plug-in completion) can \fct{predict}
complete another \class{claims\_panel} with the same features, factor levels and horizon.

Its random numbers come from the \code{"L'Ecuyer-CMRG"} generator
\citep{LEcuyer+Simard+Chen+Kelton:2002}. Replicate $b$ receives stream $b$ after the seed, and
development year $l$ receives substream $l + 1$ within that stream; a worker sets
that state before drawing a bootstrap sample. Under the same software, the completed replicates are therefore identical for any number of workers, any
batching of replicates and any scheduling, and the first $B$ replicates of a larger run are those
of a run with $B$ replicates; the out-of-bag sums are accumulated batch by batch and can differ in
their last floating-point digits. An explicit \code{seed} leaves the caller's generator kind and
state unchanged; with \code{seed = NULL} the seed is drawn from it. \Sexpr{rng_tree_txt} The source
article's code drew its bootstrap samples with \fct{bagging} from \pkg{ipred} under a different
seeding scheme, so its replicates cannot be reproduced exactly. Parallel execution uses forked
processes on Unix (\pkg{parallel}) and a socket cluster elsewhere. Socket workers are fresh
\proglang{R} sessions: they receive the variables of the global environment and the attached
packages that the functions of a user-supplied learner refer to (found with \pkg{codetools}), so
that a learner written at top level gives the same replicates on every backend; one that reaches
an object only by name, through \fct{get} say, fails on them with a message saying so.

\subsection{Practical use} \label{sec:practical}

<<practical, echo=FALSE>>=
cov_B <- function(B) 1 - (1 - exp(-1))^B
bss_file <- "results/robustness/bagging_seeds_summary.csv"; have_bss <- file.exists(bss_file) && file.exists(seeds_file)
if (have_bss) {
  bss <- read.csv(bss_file); bsv <- function(stat, col) bss[[col]][bss$statistic == stat]
  bseeds <- read.csv(seeds_file)
  rsd <- function(h) bsv("mean replicate sd", h) / bsv("mean of means", h)
  live_rsd <- if (have_live) { v <- calendar_year_payments(res_live, "total"); c(sd(v[1, ]) / mean(v[1, ]), sd(v["total", ]) / mean(v["total", ])) } else c(NA, NA)
  live_mc <- if (have_live) sprintf(" (%s and %s for that of the live run alone, \\supp{2})",
                                     pct(live_rsd[1] / sqrt(res_live$B), 2), pct(live_rsd[2] / sqrt(res_live$B), 2)) else ""
  B_txt <- sprintf("Over %d bagging seeds with $B = %d$ on the shipped portfolio, the bagged means vary with a standard deviation of %s of their mean for next year and %s for the total through development year 11, of the order of the replicate standard deviation divided by $\\sqrt{B}$: %s and %s for the replicate standard deviation averaged over the %d seeds%s.",
    nrow(bseeds), bseeds$B[1], pct(bsv("sd of means relative to mean of means", "next_year"), 2), pct(bsv("sd of means relative to mean of means", "total"), 2),
    pct(rsd("next_year") / sqrt(bseeds$B[1]), 2), pct(rsd("total") / sqrt(bseeds$B[1]), 2), nrow(bseeds), live_mc)
} else B_txt <- missing_txt("results/robustness/bagging\\_seeds*.csv")
bench_file <- "results/engine_benchmark/table_benchmark.csv"; have_bench <- file.exists(bench_file)
if (have_bench) {
  bm <- read.csv(bench_file)
  stopifnot(bm$retained_mb[2] > 100 * bm$retained_mb[1])   # the prose below
  bench_txt <- sprintf("On the shipped portfolio, a straightforward implementation that fits \\fct{bagging} from \\pkg{ipred} for each development year (\\code{keepX = FALSE}, \\code{coob = FALSE}, the same \\fct{rpart.control}) and keeps its trees, with the components \\pkg{rpart} stores by default, holds %s~GB for $B = %d$ replicates, against %s~MB for \\fct{bag\\_reserve} with its default \\code{keep\\_trees = FALSE} (\\code{replication/\\allowbreak engine\\_benchmark.R}); on one worker it took %s~s, \\fct{bag\\_reserve} %s~s, which also computes the out-of-bag diagnostic and the claim-level reserve. With \\code{keep\\_trees = TRUE} the package keeps all $B \\times L$ unpruned trees as well, and its memory then grows linearly in $B$ in the same way. ",
    formatC(bm$retained_mb[2] / 1024, format = "f", digits = 1), bm$B[1], formatC(bm$retained_mb[1], format = "f", digits = 0),
    fmt(bm$elapsed_secs[2]), fmt(bm$elapsed_secs[1]))
} else bench_txt <- ""
scale_txt <- if (file.exists(timing_file)) {
  tm0 <- read.csv(timing_file); ag0 <- aggregate(elapsed_secs ~ ncores, tm0, mean)
  sprintf("In the timing study of \\supp{2} the time per replicate falls nearly in proportion to the number of workers, %s times faster on %d workers than on one; the other deposited fits ran under other loads.",
          formatC(ag0$elapsed_secs[1] / ag0$elapsed_secs[nrow(ag0)], format = "f", digits = 1), max(ag0$ncores))
} else missing_txt(timing_file)
@
The main choices are the completion, $B$, $L$, \code{keep\_trees} and the number of workers
(Table~\ref{tab:defaults} lists the key arguments and defaults). The default \code{completion =
"mean"} reproduces the source article and is kept for that reason; for totals beyond next year use
\code{completion = "draw"}, as in the call \code{bag\_reserve(panel, B, seed, completion =
"draw")} of Section~\ref{sec:portfolio}, and back-test the result at several valuation dates
(Section~\ref{sec:revalue}).

\emph{The number of replicates.} The Monte Carlo error of the bagged mean is the replicate
standard deviation divided by $\sqrt{B}$. \Sexpr{B_txt} Replicates in the hundreds therefore fix
the bagged mean far more precisely than the back-tests of Section~\ref{sec:evaluation} measure
its error, and the default $B = 100$ suffices for it; the extreme replicate quantiles need more
(\supp{2} compares $B = \Sexpr{B_live_txt}$ with $B = \Sexpr{B_full_txt}$), and the out-of-bag
coverage needs $B$ well above ten (it is about \Sexpr{pct(cov_B(10), 1)} at $B = 10$).

\emph{The horizon.} \code{L} is never inferred. The last tree needs training claims with $a_r +
L \le t$, so $L$ is at most the number of accident years less one when the latest accident year
is $t$; \fct{backtest} reports the payments beyond $L$ as the excluded tail.

\emph{Memory.} \Sexpr{bench_txt}

\emph{Workers.} \code{ncores} sets the number of worker processes, at most the number of cores
detected (a larger request is reduced, with a message; it is used as given when that number
cannot be detected); \code{backend = "auto"} forks on
Unix, where the workers share the panel, and starts a socket cluster elsewhere, where each
receives a copy. The results do not depend on these choices (Section~\ref{sec:storage}).
\Sexpr{scale_txt}

<<defaults-table, echo=FALSE, results="asis">>=
ns <- asNamespace("baggingReserve")
key_args <- list(claims_panel = c("t", "L", "features", "accident_years", "na_features", "future_payments", "beyond_L"),
                 reserve_tree = c("control", "prune", "seed", "learner"),
                 bag_reserve = c("B", "seed", "ncores", "control", "prune", "oob", "keep_trees", "batch_size", "backend", "claim_level", "learner", "completion"),
                 predict.reserve_tree = c("newdata", "type", "completion", "R", "seed"),
                 ibnr_reserve = c("counts", "projection", "round"),
                 summary.reserve_prediction = c("probs", "type"),
                 backtest = c("probs", "type", "rep_year", "reference", "reference_component", "observed_to"),
                 chain_ladder = c("input", "projection"),
                 two_part_learner = c("occurrence", "size", "min_nonzero"))
show_arg <- function(f, a) {
  fm <- formals(get(f, envir = ns))
  if (!a %in% names(fm)) return(sprintf("\\textbf{[%s: no argument %s]}", gsub("_", "\\\\_", f), gsub("_", "\\\\_", a)))
  v <- if (is.character(fm[[a]])) deparse(fm[[a]]) else as.character(fm[a])   # "" without a default; strings quoted
  txt <- if (!nzchar(v)) a else paste(a, "=", v)
  paste0("\\code{", gsub("_", "\\\\_", txt), "}")
}
label_of <- c(predict.reserve_tree = "predict()} on a \\class{reserve\\_tree", summary.reserve_prediction = "summary()} on a \\class{reserve\\_prediction")
rows <- vapply(names(key_args), function(f) {
  nm <- if (f %in% names(label_of)) paste0("\\code{", label_of[[f]], "}") else paste0("\\fct{", gsub("_", "\\\\_", f), "}")
  paste0(nm, " & ", paste(vapply(key_args[[f]], function(a) show_arg(f, a), ""), collapse = ", "), " \\\\")
}, "")
cat("\\begin{table}[t!]\n\\centering\n\\footnotesize\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{3.4cm}>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{10.9cm}@{}}\n\\toprule\nFunction & Key arguments and their defaults \\\\\n\\midrule\n")
cat(rows, sep = "\n")
cat(sprintf("\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Key arguments of the main functions with their defaults, read from \\pkg{baggingReserve} %s (an argument without a default must be supplied; for a vector of choices, the first is the default). \\code{predict()} on a \\class{bag\\_reserve} takes only \\code{newdata} and \\code{type}: its completion is that of \\fct{bag\\_reserve}. The column names of \\fct{backtest}, \\code{rep\\_year} among them, default to those given to the prediction's \\fct{claims\\_panel}.}\n\\label{tab:defaults}\n\\end{table}\n", packageVersion("baggingReserve")))
@


%% =============================================================================
\section{Illustration} \label{sec:illustration}

\subsection{The data contract on a toy portfolio} \label{sec:toy}

Five claims and six transactions illustrate the rules. Claim \code{d} is reported after the
valuation date $t = 3$ and is not part of the panel; claim \code{a} has two payments in year 1
and a recovery in year 2; claim \code{b} is reported one year after its accident, so its cell
for development year 0 is an observed zero; claim \code{e} is reported but has no payment yet;
the cell for development year 2 of claim \code{c} lies in the future.
%
<<toy>>=
library("baggingReserve")
claims <- data.frame(Id = c("a", "b", "c", "d", "e"),
  AccYear = c(1, 1, 2, 3, 3), RepYear = c(1, 2, 2, 4, 3),
  Type = c("x", "y", "x", "y", "x"))
payments <- data.frame(Id = c("a", "a", "a", "b", "c", "d"),
  EventYear = c(1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 4), Paid = c(10, 5, -2, 7, 3, 1))
panel <- claims_panel(claims, payments, t = 3, L = 2, features = "Type")
panel
cbind(panel$claims, panel$payments)
as_triangle(panel)
@
%
A payment after the valuation date triggers an error unless it is dropped explicitly:
%
<<toy-error, error=TRUE>>=
late <- rbind(payments, data.frame(Id = "a", EventYear = 4, Paid = 9))
msg <- tryCatch(claims_panel(claims, late, t = 3, L = 2, features = "Type"),
                error = conditionMessage)
writeLines(strwrap(msg, width = 70))
claims_panel(claims, late, t = 3, L = 2, features = "Type",
             future_payments = "drop")$diagnostics$future_amount_dropped
@

\subsection{The shipped synthetic portfolio} \label{sec:portfolio}

The package ships a synthetic portfolio from the simulator of \citet{Wang+Wuthrich:2022}, built
on \pkg{SynthETIC} and run on a yearly grid with seed 2026: six claim types, twelve accident
years, valuation at $t = 12$, horizon $L = 11$. It is a fresh portfolio from the simulator used
by the source article, not the source article's own, so the numbers below do not attempt to
reproduce that article's. \code{synthetic\_claims} and \code{synthetic\_payments} hold what the
insurer knows at the valuation date; \code{synthetic\_claims\_full} and
\code{synthetic\_payments\_full} add the generated ultimate amounts and the payments after the
valuation date, for back-testing. The trees see the type, the age, the weekday of the accident
and the reporting delay, together with the payment history; the ultimate claim size, which
drives the payments, is withheld. The generator violates the model of Section~\ref{sec:method}
in several ways (inflation, a drifting settlement delay, a claim-size cycle in the accident
date, and late-reported claims that are smaller on average), and on the yearly grid its calendar
conversion still treats a time unit as a month, which leaves artefacts in the weekday feature and
the reporting delay; the back-tests below therefore measure the method under misspecification
(\supp{1} describes the generator and these mechanisms).
%
<<data>>=
data("synthetic_claims", "synthetic_payments", package = "baggingReserve")
panel <- claims_panel(synthetic_claims, synthetic_payments, t = 12, L = 11,
                      features = c("Type", "Age", "AccWeekday"))
panel
@
%
<<delay-columns, echo=FALSE>>=
cnt_cols <- which(colSums(claim_counts(panel), na.rm = TRUE) > 0) - 1L
empty_delays <- setdiff(0:max(panel$claims$rep_delay), cnt_cols)
empty_note <- if (length(empty_delays)) sprintf(" (the column for delay %s is empty)", paste(empty_delays, collapse = ", ")) else ""
@
In the triangle of reporting counts, \code{claim\_counts(panel)}, the reporting delay runs from 0 to \Sexpr{max(panel$claims$rep_delay)} years:
of the \Sexpr{max(panel$claims$rep_delay) + 1L} delay columns in that range, \Sexpr{length(cnt_cols)} contain
claims\Sexpr{empty_note}; \Sexpr{fmt(panel$diagnostics$n_claims_without_payment)} reported
claims have no payment yet and \Sexpr{panel$diagnostics$n_recoveries} of the transactions are
recoveries. Fitting one pruned tree per development year takes less than a minute:
%
<<tree>>=
tree <- reserve_tree(panel, seed = 1)
tree
res_tree <- ibnr_reserve(predict(tree), claim_counts(panel))
calendar_year_payments(res_tree, "total")[c("13", "total"), ]
@
%
<<roots, echo=FALSE, results="asis">>=
leaves <- vapply(tree$fits, function(f) sum(f$frame$var == "<leaf>"), 1L)
roots <- which(leaves == 1L) - 1L
and_list <- function(x) if (length(x) > 1) paste0(paste(x[-length(x)], collapse = ", "), " and ", x[length(x)]) else as.character(x)
if (length(roots)) cat(sprintf("The tree%s of period%s %s %s pruned to %s root node%s: the response is mostly zero and no split survives cross-validation, so %s predict%s the training mean for every claim.\n",
  if (length(roots) > 1) "s" else "", if (length(roots) > 1) "s" else "", and_list(roots), if (length(roots) > 1) "are" else "is",
  if (length(roots) > 1) "their" else "its", if (length(roots) > 1) "s" else "",
  if (length(roots) > 1) "these trees" else "this tree", if (length(roots) > 1) "" else "s"))
@

A bagged fit with 50 replicates takes a few minutes on two worker processes:
<<bag-small, eval=have_live>>=
bag <- bag_reserve(panel, B = 50, seed = 2026, ncores = 2)
res50 <- ibnr_reserve(predict(bag))
summary(claim_reserves(res50)$reserve)
@
The last line summarises the reserves of the individual reported claims. The replication script
makes the same call with $B = \Sexpr{B_live_txt}$ replicates and 8 worker processes. Replicate
$b$ always uses random-number stream $b$ (Section~\ref{sec:storage}), so the first 50 of those
replicates are the ones just computed; the next chunk reads that deposit, which is part of the
replication material (\code{results/live/}), not of the package:
<<live-check, echo=FALSE, results="asis">>=
if (!have_live) cat("\\textbf{[The live deposit results/live/ is missing; run paper/replication/run\\_all.R.]}\n")
@
<<live-print, eval=have_live>>=
res <- readRDS("results/live/reserve_prediction_live.rds")
c(reported = all.equal(res50$reported, res$reported[, 1:50]),
  ibnr = all.equal(res50$ibnr, res$ibnr[, 1:50]))
smry <- summary(res, probs = c(0.025, 0.5, 0.975))
round(smry$tables$total[c("13", "total"), c("mean", "sd", "q2.5%", "q97.5%")])
@
%
<<live-timing, echo=FALSE, results="asis">>=
if (have_live) { oob <- readRDS("results/live/bagging_live.rds")$oob   # the out-of-bag table of the deposit
  stopifnot(all(oob$coverage == 1)) }   # "every training claim receives out-of-bag predictions"
@
Every training claim receives out-of-bag predictions (the deposit holds the out-of-bag table). The out-of-bag scores are low, between
$\Sexpr{if (have_live) formatC(min(oob$oob_score), format = "f", digits = 2) else "?"}$ and
$\Sexpr{if (have_live) formatC(max(oob$oob_score), format = "f", digits = 2) else "?"}$, and
negative in the later development years: the one-year regressions explain little of the
variance of next year's payment given the history. The predicted
payments of the last calendar years are small and can be negative, because a few recoveries
make the training mean of a late tree negative (\supp{2}).

The uncensored data allow a back-test against the payments that actually followed;
Figure~\ref{fig:calendar} shows the predicted payments by calendar year alongside the realised
payments, and Table~\ref{tab:bench} summarises the point predictions:
%
<<backtest-fit, eval=have_live, together=TRUE>>=
data("synthetic_claims_full", "synthetic_payments_full",
  package = "baggingReserve")
bt <- backtest(res, synthetic_claims_full, synthetic_payments_full)
d <- as.data.frame(bt)
d <- d[d$calendar %in% c("13", "total"), ]
transform(d[, c("component", "calendar", "truth", "predicted")],
  truth = round(truth), predicted = round(predicted),
  rel_error = round(d$rel_error, 3))
@
<<truth-cal, eval=have_live, echo=FALSE>>=
truth_cal <- setNames(bt$tables$total$truth, bt$tables$total$calendar)
@

<<calendar, eval=have_live, echo=FALSE, fig.width=7, fig.height=3.2, out.width="0.6\\textwidth", fig.cap=paste0("Predicted payments per future calendar year for all claims: the mean over the $B = ", B_live_txt, "$ replicates (line and dots), the shaded interval between the 2.5\\% and 97.5\\% replicate quantiles (type 7; resampling variation only) and the realised payments (crosses).")>>=
plot(res, "calendar", truth = truth_cal, main = "")
@

<<bench, eval=have_live, echo=FALSE, results="asis">>=
bench <- read.csv("results/live/table_benchmarks.csv", check.names = FALSE); bench_raw <- bench
bench$next_year_rel_error <- pct(bench$next_year_rel_error); bench$ultimate_rel_error <- pct(bench$ultimate_rel_error)
bench <- bench[, c("method", "next_year", "next_year_rel_error", "ultimate", "ultimate_rel_error")]
bench$method <- sentence(bench$method)
names(bench) <- c("Method", "Payments", "Rel. error", "Payments", "Rel. error")
cat("\\begin{table}[t!]\n\\centering\n\\small\n")
latex_table_grouped(bench, groups = list(list(label = "Next year", span = 2L), list(label = "Through development year 11", span = 2L)),
                    sub = c("Payments", "Rel. error", "Payments", "Rel. error"), align = "lrrrr")
cat(sprintf("\\caption{Predicted payments for all claims in the next year (calendar year 13) and in all future years through development year 11, and their relative errors with respect to the realised payments: the single-tree fit, the bagged mean over $B = %d$ replicates (plug-in completion, the default) and the standard chain ladder on the paid triangle.}\n\\label{tab:bench}\n\\end{table}\n", res$B))
@

<<components, eval=have_live, echo=FALSE>>=
n_tot <- nrow(bt$tables$total)
rep1 <- bt$tables$reported[1, ]; ibnr1 <- bt$tables$ibnr[1, ]; tot1 <- bt$tables$total[1, ]
repT <- bt$tables$reported[n_tot, ]; ibnrT <- bt$tables$ibnr[n_tot, ]; totT <- bt$tables$total[n_tot, ]
where_total <- if (totT$truth < totT$lower) "below the 2.5\\% replicate quantile" else
  if (totT$truth > totT$upper) "above the 97.5\\% replicate quantile" else "between the 2.5\\% and 97.5\\% replicate quantiles"
cy_res <- calendar_year_payments(res, "total")
@
<<by-ay, eval=have_live, echo=FALSE>>=
# all future payments through development year L by accident year: realised, bagged mean, chain ladder
# (the table is in the supplement, Section S2)
pf <- synthetic_payments_full; cf <- synthetic_claims_full
pay_ay <- cf$AccYear[match(pf$Id, cf$Id)]; dev <- pf$EventYear - pay_ay
fut <- pf$EventYear > res$t & dev <= res$L
ays <- sort(unique(res$cells$acc_year))
truth_ay <- tapply(pf$Paid[fut], factor(pay_ay[fut], levels = ays), sum)
bag_ay <- tapply(rowMeans(res$reported + res$ibnr), factor(res$cells$acc_year, levels = ays), sum)
cl_live <- readRDS("results/live/chain_ladder.rds"); cl_ay <- cl_live$outstanding[as.character(ays)]
stopifnot(isTRUE(all.equal(sum(truth_ay), totT$truth)), isTRUE(all.equal(sum(bag_ay), totT$predicted)))
tr <- as.numeric(truth_ay); sae_bag <- sum(abs(as.numeric(bag_ay) - tr)); sae_cl <- sum(abs(as.numeric(cl_ay) - tr))
n_closer <- sum(abs(as.numeric(bag_ay) - tr) < abs(as.numeric(cl_ay) - tr))
stopifnot(sae_bag > sae_cl, abs(totT$rel_error) < abs(bench_raw$ultimate_rel_error[bench_raw$method == "chain ladder"]))
@
The back-test holds one table per component (all of it is printed in \supp{2}). Next year's
total is \Sexpr{signed(tot1$rel_error)}; payments for reported claims are
\Sexpr{signed(rep1$rel_error)} and payments for IBNR claims
\Sexpr{signed(ibnr1$rel_error)}\Sexpr{net_of(rep1$error, ibnr1$error)}. The total through
development year 11 is \Sexpr{signed(totT$rel_error)} (reported-claim payments
\Sexpr{signed(repT$rel_error)}, IBNR payments \Sexpr{signed(ibnrT$rel_error)}). The realised
total lies \Sexpr{where_total}\Sexpr{if (totT$truth >= totT$lower && totT$truth <= totT$upper) ", " else "; the 2.5\\% and 97.5\\% replicate quantiles are "}\Sexpr{fmt(totT$lower)}
and \Sexpr{fmt(totT$upper)}. Next year's realised payments, \Sexpr{fmt(tot1$truth)},
\Sexpr{if (tot1$truth > max(cy_res[1, ])) "exceed all" else if (tot1$truth < min(cy_res[1, ])) "fall below all" else "lie within the range of the"}
\Sexpr{res$B} replicate predictions, which range from \Sexpr{fmt(min(cy_res[1, ]))} to
\Sexpr{fmt(max(cy_res[1, ]))}: the replicate spread measures resampling variation, not forecast
error (Section~\ref{sec:bagging}). The chain ladder's next-year absolute relative error of
\Sexpr{pct(abs(bench_raw$next_year_rel_error[bench_raw$method == "chain ladder"]))} is smaller
than the bagged mean's \Sexpr{pct(abs(tot1$rel_error))}; for the total through development year
11 the bagged mean is closer, but by offsetting errors across accident years: it is closer than
the chain ladder for \Sexpr{n_closer} of the \Sexpr{length(ays)} accident years, and summed in
absolute value over the accident years its errors amount to \Sexpr{fmt(sae_bag)}, against
\Sexpr{fmt(sae_cl)} for the chain ladder (\supp{2} gives the breakdown); a back-test should look
at it as well as at the total. Nor does its small error for the total, against the single-tree fit's
\Sexpr{pct(abs(bench_raw$ultimate_rel_error[bench_raw$method == "single tree"]))}, show that
averaging corrects the single tree: the fits differ in pruning as well, and on every further
generator portfolio the bagged mean overpredicts the total by more (\supp{4}).

<<ibnr-counts, eval=have_live, echo=FALSE, results="asis">>=
# The IBNR error split into the error of the projected counts and that of the payment per
# late-reported claim: results/live/ibnr_counts.csv (acc_year, projected, realised) holds the
# chain-ladder projection of the claims reported after t and the claims actually reported later.
ic_file <- "results/live/ibnr_counts.csv"
if (!file.exists(ic_file)) {
  cat("The IBNR component is the product of projected counts and a pooled mean payment, so its error has two sources: the count projection and the payment per late-reported claim.", missing_txt(ic_file), "\n\n")
} else {
  ic <- read.csv(ic_file, stringsAsFactors = FALSE); stopifnot(all(c("acc_year", "projected", "realised") %in% names(ic)))
  ic <- ic[ic$acc_year != "total", ]; ic$acc_year <- as.integer(ic$acc_year)   # accident years only, without the total row
  e_cnt <- sum(ic$projected) / sum(ic$realised) - 1
  e_sev <- (1 + ibnrT$rel_error) / (1 + e_cnt) - 1           # through development year L
  ic_last <- ic[which.max(ic$acc_year), ]
  cat(sprintf("The IBNR component is the product of projected counts and a pooled mean payment, so its error has two sources. The chain ladder on the reporting counts projects %s claims to be reported after the valuation date, against %s actually reported later, so the counts are %s; given this count error, the IBNR payments through development year 11, %s, imply that the payment per late-reported claim is %s ($(1 + e_I)/(1 + e_N) - 1$, with $e_I$ and $e_N$ the relative errors of the payments and of the counts), the error of the pooled mean of Equation~\\eqref{eq:mu} on a generator whose late-reported claims are smaller (\\supp{1}).\n\n",
    fmt(sum(ic$projected)), fmt(sum(ic$realised)), signed(e_cnt), signed(ibnrT$rel_error), signed(e_sev)))
}
@

For totals we recommend simulated completion (Section~\ref{sec:learners}); the call differs in one
argument, here with the same 50 replicates as above:
<<bag-draw, eval=have_live, together=TRUE>>=
bag_draw <- bag_reserve(panel, B = 50, seed = 2026, ncores = 2,
  completion = "draw")
bt_draw <- backtest(ibnr_reserve(predict(bag_draw)),
  synthetic_claims_full, synthetic_payments_full)
d_draw <- bt_draw$tables$total[c(1, nrow(bt_draw$tables$total)), ]
@
<<bag-draw-text, eval=have_live, echo=FALSE>>=
draw_cmp <- if (d_draw$rel_error[2] < 0 && totT$rel_error > 0) "turns the overprediction of the plug-in total into an underprediction" else
  if (abs(d_draw$rel_error[2]) < abs(totT$rel_error)) "reduces the error of the plug-in total" else "does not reduce the error of the plug-in total"
@
On this misspecified portfolio simulated completion \Sexpr{draw_cmp} (\Sexpr{signed(d_draw$rel_error[2])}, against
\Sexpr{signed(totT$rel_error)} with plugged-in means and $B = \Sexpr{res$B}$; \supp{7}); on a
correctly specified generator it removes the bias of the plug-in recursion
(Section~\ref{sec:well-specified}).

\subsection{Back-testing at earlier valuation dates} \label{sec:revalue}

A user's own data have no realised future, but they can be back-tested by valuing them at an
earlier date: the claims of the accident years up to the new valuation date are kept, the
constructor keeps only those reported by \code{t}, \code{future\_payments = "drop"} sets aside
the payments after \code{t}, which then serve as the truth, and the argument \code{reference} of
\fct{backtest} scores the chain ladder against the same truth. On the shipped portfolio, valued
one year earlier:
<<revalue>>=
cl11 <- synthetic_claims_full[synthetic_claims_full$AccYear <= 11, ]
pay11 <- synthetic_payments_full[synthetic_payments_full$Id %in% cl11$Id, ]
panel11 <- claims_panel(cl11, pay11, t = 11, L = 10,
  features = c("Type", "Age", "AccWeekday"), future_payments = "drop")
bt11 <- backtest(ibnr_reserve(predict(reserve_tree(panel11, seed = 1))),
  cl11, pay11, reference = chain_ladder(as_triangle(panel11)))
d11 <- as.data.frame(bt11)
d11 <- d11[d11$component == "total" & d11$calendar %in% c("12", "total"), ]
print(data.frame(calendar = d11$calendar, realised = round(d11$truth),
  tree = round(d11$predicted), chain_ladder = round(d11$reference)),
  row.names = FALSE)
@
<<revalue-check, echo=FALSE>>=
# the displayed workflow gives the single-tree numbers of the deposit of replication/valuation_t11.R
if (file.exists("results/t11/table_benchmarks.csv")) {
  tb11_chk <- read.csv("results/t11/table_benchmarks.csv")
  stopifnot(isTRUE(all.equal(d11$predicted, unlist(tb11_chk[tb11_chk$method == "single tree", c("next_year", "ultimate")]), check.attributes = FALSE)),
            isTRUE(all.equal(d11$reference, unlist(tb11_chk[tb11_chk$method == "chain ladder", c("next_year", "ultimate")]), check.attributes = FALSE)))
}
@
<<t11, eval=file.exists("results/t11/table_benchmarks.csv"), echo=FALSE, results="asis">>=
tb11 <- read.csv("results/t11/table_benchmarks.csv"); tc11 <- read.csv("results/t11/table_components.csv")
rownames(tc11) <- tc11$component
ri11 <- read.csv("results/t11/run_info.csv"); r11 <- setNames(ri11$value, ri11$quantity)
rel11 <- function(m, h) tb11[[paste0(h, "_rel_error")]][tb11$method == m]
best11 <- function(h) { e <- c(`the bagged mean` = abs(rel11("bagging (mean)", h)), `the single-tree fit` = abs(rel11("single tree", h)),
                                `the chain ladder` = abs(rel11("chain ladder", h))); names(e)[which.min(e)] }
cat(sprintf("With $L = %d$, the largest horizon with training claims for its last tree, the script \\code{replication/\\allowbreak valuation\\_t11.R} adds the bagged fit ($B = %d$): next year's payments are %s by the bagged mean, %s by the single-tree fit and %s by the chain ladder, and the total through development year %d is %s, %s and %s, respectively. %s\n\n",
  as.integer(r11[["L"]]), as.integer(r11[["B"]]),
  signed(rel11("bagging (mean)", "next_year")), signed(rel11("single tree", "next_year")), signed(rel11("chain ladder", "next_year")),
  as.integer(r11[["L"]]),
  signed(rel11("bagging (mean)", "ultimate")), signed(rel11("single tree", "ultimate")), signed(rel11("chain ladder", "ultimate")),
  if (best11("next_year") == best11("ultimate")) sprintf("The closest predictor is %s at both horizons.", best11("ultimate")) else sprintf("The closest predictor is %s for next year and %s for the total.", best11("next_year"), best11("ultimate"))))
@
<<rolling, eval=have_rev && file.exists(file.path(rev_dir, "rolling_backtests.csv")), echo=FALSE, results="asis">>=
rl <- read.csv(file.path(rev_dir, "rolling_backtests.csv")); rcf <- read.csv(file.path(rev_dir, "config.csv"))
roll_B <- as.integer(rcf$value[rcf$key == "ROLL_B"])
mae <- function(v) pct(mean(abs(v))); smean <- function(v) { m <- mean(v); paste0(if (m < 0) "$-$" else "", pct(abs(m))) }
# the realised value relative to the bagged mean, compared with the replicate quantiles relative to it
inside <- function(e, lo, hi) { r <- 1 / (1 + e) - 1; r >= lo & r <= hi }
in_next <- sum(inside(rl$bag_next_rel, rl$bag_next_q_lo, rl$bag_next_q_hi))
in_tot <- sum(inside(rl$bag_total_rel, rl$bag_total_q_lo, rl$bag_total_q_hi))
cat(sprintf("Repeated at several valuation dates, the same workflow gives an empirical distribution of the prediction errors, which the replicate quantiles cannot. The script \\code{replication/\\allowbreak revision\\_studies.R} back-tests the single-tree fit, a bagged fit with $B = %d$ replicates and the chain ladder at $t = %d, \\ldots, %d$, each with horizon $L = t - 1$ (\\supp{2} tabulates the errors). Over the %d valuation dates the mean absolute relative error of the total is %s for the bagged mean, %s for the single-tree fit and %s for the chain ladder (Table~\\ref{tab:evaluation})%s. The dates share claims and payments, and the horizon changes with $t$, so these errors are dependent and descriptive. The 2.5\\%% to 97.5\\%% replicate range of the bagged fit contains the realised payments at %d of the %d dates next year and %d for the total.",
  roll_B, min(rl$t), max(rl$t), nrow(rl), mae(rl$bag_total_rel), mae(rl$tree_total_rel), mae(rl$cl_total_rel),
  if (all(rl$bag_total_rel > 0)) "; the bagged mean overpredicts the total at every date" else "", in_next, nrow(rl), in_tot))
if ("draw_total_inside" %in% names(rl)) {
  stopifnot(abs(mean(rl$draw_total_rel)) < mean(rl$bag_total_rel) / 2, any(rl$draw_total_rel < 0), any(rl$draw_total_rel > 0))   # the prose
  cat(sprintf(" Simulated completion on the same trees removes most of the systematic overprediction (mean signed error of the total %s, against %s), but the errors of single dates remain large and of either sign.\n\n",
    smean(rl$draw_total_rel), smean(rl$bag_total_rel)))
} else cat("\n\n")
@

\subsection{An illustrative motor third-party liability dataset} \label{sec:mtpl}

A dataset from insurance practice shows how the software handles transaction-level data: the
motor third-party liability (MTPL) claims published in a public code repository
\citep{rfpipeline}, an illustrative subset of an insurer's data drawn in an undocumented way
(shipped with the replication material under its MIT licence), with one row per payment
transaction (claim identifier, claim type, accident, reporting and payment dates, amount).
We take accident years 2000 to 2009, value the claims at the end of 2009 with $L = 9$ and use the
claim type and the accident month as features; the extract ends in 2019, so every predicted cell
has its realised value. The script \code{replication/\allowbreak mtpl\_case.R} runs the workflow of
Section~\ref{sec:portfolio} on it (\supp{8} shows the calls).
<<mtpl-check, echo=FALSE, results="asis">>=
if (!have_mtpl) cat("\\textbf{[The MTPL deposit results/mtpl/ is missing; run paper/replication/mtpl\\_case.R.]}\n")
@
<<mtpl-numbers, eval=have_mtpl, echo=FALSE>>=
md <- read.csv(file.path(mtpl_dir, "data_summary.csv")); mv <- setNames(md$value, md$quantity)
mt <- read.csv(file.path(mtpl_dir, "claim_types.csv"))
mb <- read.csv(file.path(mtpl_dir, "table_benchmarks.csv"), check.names = FALSE)
mc <- read.csv(file.path(mtpl_dir, "table_components.csv")); rownames(mc) <- mc$component
mo <- read.csv(file.path(mtpl_dir, "table_oob.csv"))
mtc <- read.csv(file.path(mtpl_dir, "tree_cp.csv"))
mfit <- readRDS(file.path(mtpl_dir, "mtpl_fit.rds"))
mpd <- read.csv(file.path(mtpl_dir, "panel_diagnostics.csv"))
share_of <- function(type) mt$share[mt$Type == type]
mroots <- mtc$period[mtc$leaves == 1]
mrel <- function(m, h) mb[[paste0(h, "_rel_error")]][mb$method == m]
verb <- function(e) if (e < 0) paste("underpredicts by", pct(-e)) else paste("overpredicts by", pct(e))
verb2 <- function(e1, e2) if (sign(e1) == sign(e2)) paste(if (e1 < 0) "underpredicts by" else "overpredicts by", pct(abs(e1)), "and", paste0(pct(abs(e2)), ", respectively")) else paste(verb(e1), "and", verb(e2))
mtail <- mv[["amount, dev > L"]] / mv[["amount after t, dev <= L"]]   # tail beyond L relative to the reserve target
@

<<mtpl-short, eval=have_mtpl, echo=FALSE, results="asis">>=
mr_file <- "results/mtpl_rolling/table_rolling.csv"; have_mr <- file.exists(mr_file)
cat(sprintf("The extract has no claims register, so the claims table is reconstructed from the transactions through %d: %s of the %s claims reported by 2009 that have no transaction by then are in the data only because they transact later, and the claim type may have been assigned after $t$. The trees and the IBNR counts are thus conditioned on information from after $t$ (\\supp{8}), and the results illustrate the software, not the accuracy of the method. ",
  as.integer(mv[["max payment year"]]), fmt(mpd$n_claims_without_payment), fmt(mpd$n_reported)))
cat(sprintf("Its results are in Table~\\ref{tab:evaluation} and \\supp{8}. The IBNR component %s through development year 9: a claim reported after the valuation date goes on to pay %s on average, against %s for a claim reported by then, a difference the pooled mean payment of Equation~\\eqref{eq:mu} cannot reflect.",
  verb(mc["ibnr", "total_rel_error"]),
  fmt(mc["ibnr", "total_truth"] / mv[["reported after t (IBNR)"]]), fmt(mc["reported", "total_truth"] / mv[["reported by t"]])))
if (have_mr) {
  mr <- read.csv(mr_file); mts <- sort(unique(mr$t))
  tot <- function(m) mr$total_rel[mr$method == m]
  plug <- tot("bagged mean"); sim <- tot("bagged mean, simulated completion")
  stopifnot(all(plug < 0), all(sim > plug))   # the prose: underprediction at every date, raised by simulation
  cat(sprintf(" At the %d valuation dates %d to %d (\\supp{8}, Table~\\ref{tab:evaluation}) the plug-in bagged mean underpredicts the total at every date and simulated completion raises it at every date: the plug-in recursion errs downwards here, upwards on the generator.\n\n",
    length(mts), min(mts), max(mts)))
} else cat("\n\n")
@

\subsection{What the back-tests show} \label{sec:evaluation}

<<evaluation, echo=FALSE, results="asis">>=
ev_files <- c(robust_file, file.path(rev_dir, "rolling_backtests.csv"), "results/mix_shift/table_benchmarks.csv")
if (!(have_live && have_mtpl && all(file.exists(ev_files)))) {
  cat(missing_txt("results/robustness, revision, mix_shift, mtpl or live"), "\n\n")
  ev_ok <- FALSE
} else {
  ev_ok <- TRUE
  smean <- function(v) { m <- mean(v); paste0(if (m < 0) "$-$" else "", pct(abs(m))) }   # a signed mean error
  bl <- read.csv("results/live/table_benchmarks.csv", check.names = FALSE)
  six <- function(tab, mcol, names_map, nx, tt) {   # one row of the six relative errors
    g <- function(m, col) tab[[col]][tab[[mcol]] == m]
    data.frame(tree_next = g(names_map[1], nx), tree_total = g(names_map[1], tt), bag_next = g(names_map[2], nx),
               bag_total = g(names_map[2], tt), cl_next = g(names_map[3], nx), cl_total = g(names_map[3], tt))
  }
  std <- c("single tree", "bagging (mean)", "chain ladder")
  ev_ship <- six(bl, "method", std, "next_year_rel_error", "ultimate_rel_error")
  rb <- read.csv(robust_file)
  ev_nine <- rbind(ev_ship, data.frame(tree_next = rb$tree_next_rel, tree_total = rb$tree_total_rel, bag_next = rb$bag_next_rel,
                                       bag_total = rb$bag_total_rel, cl_next = rb$cl_next_rel, cl_total = rb$cl_total_rel))
  rl <- read.csv(file.path(rev_dir, "rolling_backtests.csv"))
  ev_roll <- data.frame(tree_next = rl$tree_next_rel, tree_total = rl$tree_total_rel, bag_next = rl$bag_next_rel,
                        bag_total = rl$bag_total_rel, cl_next = rl$cl_next_rel, cl_total = rl$cl_total_rel)
  mixb <- read.csv("results/mix_shift/table_benchmarks.csv")
  ev_mix <- do.call(rbind, lapply(split(mixb, mixb$seed), six, mcol = "method", names_map = std, nx = "next_rel_error", tt = "total_rel_error"))
  mbm <- read.csv(file.path(mtpl_dir, "table_benchmarks.csv"))
  ev_mtpl <- six(mbm, "method", std, "next_year_rel_error", "ultimate_rel_error")
  # simulated completion of the bagged mean, where a deposit has it (NULL elsewhere)
  bdd <- read.csv(file.path(rev_dir, "bagging_draw.csv")); bdd <- bdd[bdd$completion == "draw" & bdd$component == "total", ]
  bagdraw_B <- as.integer(read.csv(file.path(rev_dir, "config.csv"))$value[read.csv(file.path(rev_dir, "config.csv"))$key == "BAGDRAW_B"])
  sim_ship <- data.frame(sim_next = bdd$next_mean / bdd$next_truth - 1, sim_total = bdd$total_mean / bdd$total_truth - 1)
  sim_roll <- if ("draw_total_rel" %in% names(rl)) data.frame(sim_next = rl$draw_next_rel, sim_total = rl$draw_total_rel)
  dag <- "$^\\dagger$"   # rows that are not clean back-tests (the motor claims table)
  studies <- list(list("Shipped, $t = 12$", ev_ship, sim_ship, FALSE), list(sprintf("Shipped, $t = %d$--$%d$", min(rl$t), max(rl$t)), ev_roll, sim_roll, TRUE),
                  list("Generator", ev_nine, NULL, TRUE), list("Changing mix", ev_mix, NULL, TRUE), list(paste0("Motor, $t = 2009$", dag), ev_mtpl, NULL, TRUE))
  mr_file <- "results/mtpl_rolling/table_rolling.csv"
  if (file.exists(mr_file)) {
    mr <- read.csv(mr_file)
    ev_mr <- do.call(rbind, lapply(split(mr, mr$t), six, mcol = "method", names_map = c("single tree", "bagged mean", "chain ladder"), nx = "next_rel", tt = "total_rel"))
    sim_m <- function(d) data.frame(sim_next = d$next_rel[d$method == "bagged mean, simulated completion"], sim_total = d$total_rel[d$method == "bagged mean, simulated completion"])
    sim_mr <- do.call(rbind, lapply(split(mr, mr$t), sim_m))
    # the case at 2009 is the date 2009 of the rolling study (same fit): it then contributes its simulated completion,
    # and only the four dates count as a study of their own
    m09 <- mr[mr$t == 2009, ]
    if (nrow(m09) && isTRUE(all.equal(unname(unlist(ev_mtpl)), unname(unlist(six(m09, "method", c("single tree", "bagged mean", "chain ladder"), "next_rel", "total_rel")))))) {
      studies[[5]][[3]] <- sim_m(m09); studies[[5]][[4]] <- FALSE
    }
    studies[[length(studies) + 1L]] <- list(sprintf("Motor, %d dates%s", nrow(ev_mr), dag), ev_mr, sim_mr, TRUE)
  }
  ev_tab <- do.call(rbind, lapply(studies, function(s) {
    e <- s[[2]]; a <- colMeans(abs(e)); sm <- if (is.null(s[[3]])) c(sim_next = NA, sim_total = NA) else colMeans(abs(s[[3]]))
    sp <- function(x) if (is.na(x)) "--" else pct(x)
    data.frame(study = s[[1]], n = as.character(nrow(e)),
               t1 = pct(a[["tree_next"]]), b1 = pct(a[["bag_next"]]), s1 = sp(sm[["sim_next"]]), c1 = pct(a[["cl_next"]]),
               t2 = pct(a[["tree_total"]]), b2 = pct(a[["bag_total"]]), s2 = sp(sm[["sim_total"]]), c2 = pct(a[["cl_total"]]),
               k = sprintf("%d, %d", sum(abs(e$cl_next) < abs(e$bag_next)), sum(abs(e$cl_total) < abs(e$bag_total))))
  }))
  cat("\\begin{table}[t!]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4.5pt}\n")
  latex_table_grouped(ev_tab, groups = list(list(label = "Next year", span = 4L), list(label = "Through development year $L$", span = 4L),
                                            list(label = "CL closer", span = 1L)),
                      sub = c("Tree", "Bagged", "Sim.", "CL", "Tree", "Bagged", "Sim.", "CL", "next, total"), first = "Back-test & $n$", lead = 2L, align = "llrrrrrrrrr")
  distinct <- vapply(studies, `[[`, TRUE, 4L)
  cat(sprintf("\\caption{Summary of the back-tests: mean absolute relative error of the single-tree fit, the bagged mean with the default plug-in completion (Bagged) and with simulated completion (Sim., where deposited; for the shipped portfolio at $t = 12$ with $B = %d$) and the chain ladder (CL) for all claims, over the $n$ portfolios or valuation dates of each study, and the number of them on which the chain ladder is closer than the plug-in bagged mean (CL closer). Generator: the shipped portfolio and %d further ones (\\supp{4}); changing mix: \\supp{6}; shipped, $t = 7$ to $11$: Section~\\ref{sec:revalue} and \\supp{2}; motor: Section~\\ref{sec:mtpl} and \\supp{8}. %sNot clean back-tests: the motor claims table is reconstructed from transactions after the valuation date. The shipped portfolio at $t = 12$ is one of the generator portfolios%s, and the valuation dates of one portfolio share claims and payments, so the rows are not independent; the means are descriptive. They are computed from unrounded values.}\n\\label{tab:evaluation}\n\\end{table}\n",
              bagdraw_B, nrow(rb), dag, if (sum(distinct) < length(studies) - 1L) " and the motor case at $t = 2009$ one of the motor dates" else ""))

  # --- the findings stated in the prose: counted over the distinct studies only ---
  ev_mae <- lapply(studies[distinct], function(x) colMeans(abs(x[[2]])))
  best_of <- function(a) c((a[["cl_next"]] < min(a[["tree_next"]], a[["bag_next"]])), (a[["cl_total"]] < min(a[["tree_total"]], a[["bag_total"]])))
  cl_best <- sum(vapply(ev_mae, function(a) sum(best_of(a)), 1))
  motor <- grepl("Motor", vapply(studies[distinct], `[[`, "", 1L), fixed = TRUE)
  cl_best_clean <- sum(vapply(ev_mae[!motor], function(a) sum(best_of(a)), 1))
  stopifnot(2 * cl_best > 2 * length(ev_mae), 2 * cl_best_clean > 2 * sum(!motor))   # "in most comparisons"
  n9 <- nrow(ev_nine); cl_wins <- c(sum(abs(ev_nine$cl_next) < abs(ev_nine$bag_next)), sum(abs(ev_nine$cl_total) < abs(ev_nine$bag_total)))
  stopifnot(2 * cl_wins[1] > n9, 2 * cl_wins[2] > n9)                 # "on most portfolios" (abstract, introduction)
  mixed <- function(v) any(v < 0) && any(v > 0)
  sign_txt <- if (all(ev_nine$bag_next < 0) && all(ev_nine$bag_total > 0))
    sprintf("On all %d generator portfolios the bagged mean underpredicts next year's payments and overpredicts the total%s.", n9,
            if (mixed(ev_nine$cl_next) && mixed(ev_nine$cl_total)) ", a bias of consistent sign that the chain ladder, whose errors take both signs, does not share" else "") else ""
  cat(sprintf("Table~\\ref{tab:evaluation} summarises the back-tests of the article and the supplementary material that compare all three methods. Over its %d distinct studies (%s) and the two horizons, the chain ladder has the smallest mean absolute relative error of the three methods in %d of the %d comparisons, and in %d of the %d without the motor dates; on the generator portfolios it is closer than the bagged mean on %d of the %d for next year and on %d for the total. %s These findings concern these portfolios and the default configuration, which is not tuned; the single tree is pruned and the bootstrap trees are not, so their difference is not an effect of averaging alone, and a regularised bagged configuration was not examined on them.",
    length(ev_mae), paste(tolower(sub("\\$\\^\\\\dagger\\$", "", vapply(studies[distinct], `[[`, "", 1L))), collapse = "; "),
    cl_best, 2 * length(ev_mae), cl_best_clean, 2 * sum(!motor), cl_wins[1], n9, cl_wins[2], sign_txt))
  if ("draw_total_rel" %in% names(rl))
    cat(" Part of the bias comes from the plug-in recursion, which simulated completion removes (Sections~\\ref{sec:revalue} and~\\ref{sec:well-specified}).")
  cat("\n\n")

  # the IBNR component and the replicate range on the further generator portfolios (deposited)
  ib1 <- read.csv("results/live/table_backtest_ibnr.csv")$rel_error[1]
  ibn <- rb$bag_ibnr_next_rel; nin <- sum(as.logical(rb$next_inside_range)); tin <- sum(as.logical(rb$total_inside_range)); n8 <- nrow(rb)
  w_next <- mean((rb$next_q975 - rb$next_q025) / rb$bag_next); w_tot <- mean((rb$total_q975 - rb$total_q025) / rb$bag_total)
  ib_dir <- if (all(ibn > 0)) sprintf("On every one of the %d further portfolios, the bagged mean overpredicts next year's IBNR payments, by %s to %s (%s on the shipped portfolio).", n8, pct(min(ibn)), pct(max(ibn)), pct(ib1)) else
    sprintf("The bagged mean's error for next year's IBNR payments ranges from %s to %s on the %d further portfolios (%s on the shipped portfolio).", pct(min(ibn)), pct(max(ibn)), n8, pct(ib1))
  ibt <- rb$bag_ibnr_total_rel
  why_ib <- if (all(ibn > 0)) " (The late-reported claims of this generator are smaller; Section~\\ref{sec:ibnr}.)" else ""
  cov_txt <- sprintf(" The realised next-year payments lie within the 2.5\\%% to 97.5\\%% replicate range on %d of the %d further portfolios, the realised total on %d.", nin, n8, tin)
  why_cov <- if (nin == 0 && tin == n8) sprintf(" Neither is a coverage rate (Section~\\ref{sec:bagging}): the ranges, on average %s and %s of the bagged mean, lie around a biased mean.", pct(w_next, 0), pct(w_tot, 0)) else ""
  cat(ib_dir, why_ib, cov_txt, why_cov, "\n\n", sep = "")

  # open/closed, hirem and the second portfolio, one sentence each
  oc_txt <- if (file.exists("results/open_closed/table_open_closed.csv")) {
    oc <- read.csv("results/open_closed/table_open_closed.csv"); ocv <- function(k, h, v) oc[oc$component == k & oc$horizon == h, v]
    sprintf("A good aggregate can hide a poor allocation: on the shipped portfolio, %s of the predicted reported-claim payments through development year 11 fall on claims already closed at the valuation date, and the open claims are %s (\\supp{5}).",
            pct(ocv("closed", "total", "mean") / ocv("all", "total", "mean")), signed(ocv("open", "total", "rel_error")))
  } else missing_txt("results/open\\_closed")
  hp_txt <- if (file.exists("results/hirem_portfolios/portfolios.csv")) {
    hp <- read.csv("results/hirem_portfolios/portfolios.csv")
    e_h <- hp$hirem_open_total / hp$open_truth_total - 1; e_t <- hp$tree_open_total / hp$open_truth_total - 1
    stopifnot(mean(abs(e_h)) < mean(abs(e_t)))   # the introduction: hirem is the more accurate on open claims
    sprintf(" On these open claims, the target of \\pkg{hirem}, its mean absolute relative error over the %d generator portfolios is %s, against %s for the single-tree fit (\\supp{7}).", nrow(hp), pct(mean(abs(e_h))), pct(mean(abs(e_t))))
  } else ""
  pb_txt <- ""   # the second portfolio (Section S3) is discussed in the supplement
  cat(oc_txt, hp_txt, pb_txt, "\n\n", sep = "")
}
@

\subsection{A correctly specified generator} \label{sec:well-specified}

Every generator above violates the model of Section~\ref{sec:method}, so the back-tests cannot
tell errors of the implementation from errors of the method. The script
\code{replication/\allowbreak well\_specified.R} simulates portfolios for which the method is
correctly specified and the true conditional reserve is known exactly: each of $I = 10$ accident
years has $n$ claims of three types, a reporting delay of zero to two years and an age feature
without effect. After reporting, a claim
pays in development year $l + 1$ with probability $a_{s}(k)\,\delta^{l}$, where $k$ is its type
and $s = \mathbf{1}\{X_{l} > 0\}$ records whether it paid in year $l$, and a payment is Gamma
distributed with a mean that depends on the type; nothing is paid after $L = 6$. The one-year
conditional mean is thus piecewise constant in the type and in $\mathbf{1}\{X_l > 0\}$, the same
in every accident year, and a regression tree represents it exactly. Given the information at
$t = 10$, each reported claim's expected future payments follow from an exact recursion over the
two payment states (verified by simulation and by enumerating all payment paths). Three scenarios differ only in how much a payment in one year
raises the chance of a payment in the next: strongly, moderately, or not at all ($a_1 = a_0$).
The estimators are scored on the reported claims against this truth (IBNR claims, for which the
pooling condition of Section~\ref{sec:ibnr} fails by construction, are left out). 
<<ablation, echo=FALSE>>=
# The leaf-size ablation of the bagged mean's next-year bias (replication/well_specified_ablation.R,
# a cluster job): reported from its deposit when present, else the missing-deposit marker.
abl_file <- "results/well_specified_ablation/table_summary.csv"
ablation_txt <- if (!file.exists(abl_file)) {
  paste("Whether the bias comes from the small leaves of the unpruned bootstrap trees is the question of the leaf-size ablation \\code{replication/\\allowbreak well\\_specified\\_ablation.R}.",
        missing_txt(abl_file))
} else {
  ab <- read.csv(abl_file, stringsAsFactors = FALSE)
  av <- function(cf, sc, tg = "next_year") ab[ab$config == cf & ab$scenario == sc & ab$target == tg, ]
  sb <- function(r) { v <- round(100 * r$bias, 2); paste0(if (v < 0) "$-$" else "$+$", formatC(abs(v), format = "f", digits = 2), "\\%") }
  pair <- function(cf) sprintf("%s and %s", sb(av(cf, "none")), sb(av(cf, "strong")))
  z <- function(cf, sc, tg = "next_year") { r <- av(cf, sc, tg); r$bias / r$bias_se }
  scs <- c("none", "strong"); n_abl <- unique(ab$n_per_ay); m_abl <- unique(ab$portfolios)
  stopifnot(length(n_abl) == 1L, all(c("bag_default", "bag_mb25", "bag_mb100", "bag_pruned", "tree_pruned") %in% ab$config))
  big_mb <- max(ab$minbucket[ab$config == "bag_mb100"])
  def_pos <- all(vapply(scs, function(sc) z("bag_default", sc) > 3, TRUE))
  mb_gone <- all(vapply(scs, function(sc) abs(z("bag_mb100", sc)) < 2.5, TRUE))
  mb_less <- all(vapply(scs, function(sc) av("bag_mb100", sc)$bias < 0.5 * av("bag_default", sc)$bias, TRUE))
  pr_same <- all(vapply(scs, function(sc) av("bag_pruned", sc)$bias > 0.5 * av("bag_default", sc)$bias, TRUE))
  lead <- if (def_pos && mb_gone) sprintf("The bias lies in the small leaves of the unpruned bootstrap trees: with a minimum leaf of %d claims it vanishes within Monte Carlo error", big_mb) else
    if (def_pos && mb_less) sprintf("Larger leaves of the bootstrap trees reduce the bias, to less than half with a minimum leaf of %d claims", big_mb) else
    "The leaf size of the bootstrap trees does not account for the bias"
  prune_txt <- if (pr_same) ", whereas pruning each bootstrap tree at the smallest cross-validated error of its own sample hardly changes it, presumably because copies of a claim drawn into both the training and the validation folds make the cross-validation favour large trees" else
    ", and pruning each bootstrap tree at the smallest cross-validated error of its own sample reduces it as well"
  tot_none <- if ("total" %in% ab$target) sprintf(" Without persistence the total is affected alike: %s with the default leaves, %s with a minimum leaf of %d.",
                                                   sb(av("bag_default", "none", "total")), sb(av("bag_mb100", "none", "total")), big_mb) else ""
  use_txt <- if (def_pos && (mb_gone || mb_less)) " A user of the bagged mean can therefore set a larger \\code{minbucket} in \\code{control}." else ""
  sprintf("An ablation on %d portfolios per scenario with $n = %s$ (\\code{replication/\\allowbreak well\\_specified\\_ablation.R}, \\supp{9}) varies the bootstrap trees: their next-year bias without and with strong persistence is %s with \\pkg{rpart}'s default minimum leaf of 7 claims, %s with 25, %s with %d, and %s when every bootstrap tree is pruned by cross-validation, against %s for the pruned single tree. %s%s.%s%s",
          m_abl, gsub(",", "{,}", fmt(n_abl), fixed = TRUE), pair("bag_default"), pair("bag_mb25"), pair("bag_mb100"), big_mb, pair("bag_pruned"), pair("tree_pruned"),
          lead, prune_txt, tot_none, use_txt)
}
@
<<well-specified, echo=FALSE, results="asis">>=
ws_dir <- "results/well_specified"; ws_file <- file.path(ws_dir, "table_summary.csv")
if (!file.exists(ws_file)) {
  cat(missing_txt(ws_file), "\n")
} else {
  ws <- read.csv(ws_file)
  wcfg <- read.csv(file.path(ws_dir, "config.csv")); wcfg <- setNames(wcfg$value, wcfg$key)
  sizes <- sort(unique(ws$n_per_ay)); nmax <- max(sizes)
  mfmt <- function(n) gsub(",", "{,}", fmt(n), fixed = TRUE)   # a number inside math mode
  num1 <- function(x) { v <- round(100 * x, 1); if (v == 0) v <- 0; paste0(if (v < 0) "$-$" else "", formatC(abs(v), format = "f", digits = 1)) }
  est_lab <- c(tree_plugin = "Single tree, plug-in", tree_draw = "Single tree, simulated",
               bag_plugin = "Bagged mean, plug-in", bag_draw = "Bagged mean, simulated",
               oracle_plugin = "True means, plug-in")
  scen_lab <- c(strong = "strong", moderate = "moderate", none = "none")
  cell <- function(sc, es, n, tg) ws[ws$scenario == sc & ws$estimator == es & ws$n_per_ay == n & ws$target == tg, ]
  bias_txt <- function(r) if (!nrow(r)) "" else if (is.na(r$bias_se) || r$bias_se == 0) num1(r$bias) else
    sprintf("%s (%s)", num1(r$bias), formatC(100 * r$bias_se, format = "f", digits = 1))
  rows <- list()
  for (sc in names(scen_lab)) for (es in names(est_lab)) {
    rows[[length(rows) + 1L]] <- c(Persistence = if (es == names(est_lab)[1]) scen_lab[[sc]] else "",
      Estimator = est_lab[[es]],
      setNames(vapply(sizes, function(n) bias_txt(cell(sc, es, n, "total")), ""), paste0("$n = ", vapply(sizes, mfmt, ""), "$")),
      `Next year` = bias_txt(cell(sc, es, nmax, "next_year")))
  }
  tab <- as.data.frame(do.call(rbind, rows), stringsAsFactors = FALSE, check.names = FALSE)
  cat("\\begin{table}[t!]\n\\centering\n\\small\n")
  latex_table_grouped(tab, groups = list(list(label = "Total reserve", span = length(sizes)), list(label = paste0("$n = ", mfmt(nmax), "$"), span = 1L)),
                      sub = c(paste0("$n = ", vapply(sizes, mfmt, ""), "$"), "Next year"), first = "Persistence & Estimator",
                      align = paste0("ll", strrep("r", length(sizes) + 1L)), lead = 2L)
  cat(sprintf("\\caption{\\label{tab:well-specified}Correctly specified generator: relative bias (\\%%) of the predicted payments of the reported claims against the true conditional reserve, mean over %s portfolios per size $n$ (claims per accident year; Monte Carlo standard error in parentheses), for the total through development year %s and, at the largest size, for next year's payments. Plug-in: completion by the predicted means; simulated: \\code{completion = \"draw\"}; true means, plug-in: the plug-in recursion applied to the true one-year means, without estimation. $B = %s$ replicates, $R = %s$ simulated paths for the single tree.}\n\\end{table}\n",
              wcfg[["portfolios_per_size"]], wcfg[["L"]], wcfg[["B"]], wcfg[["R_draw"]]))
  # sentences, computed from the deposit
  pk <- c("tree_plugin", "tree_draw", "bag_plugin", "bag_draw")
  nxt <- ws[ws$n_per_ay == nmax & ws$target == "next_year" & ws$estimator %in% pk, ]
  b <- function(sc, es, n = nmax, tg = "total") cell(sc, es, n, tg)$bias
  spct <- function(x) { v <- round(100 * x, 1); if (v == 0) return("0.0\\%"); paste0(if (v < 0) "$-$" else "$+$", pct(abs(x))) }   # signed percentage
  draw_max <- max(abs(ws$bias[ws$n_per_ay == nmax & ws$target == "total" & ws$estimator %in% c("tree_draw", "bag_draw")]))
  none_tree <- vapply(sizes, function(n) b("none", "tree_plugin", n), 1)
  nt <- nxt[nxt$estimator %in% c("tree_plugin", "tree_draw"), ]; nb <- nxt[nxt$estimator %in% c("bag_plugin", "bag_draw"), ]
  up2 <- function(x) paste0(formatC(ceiling(1e4 * x) / 100, format = "f", digits = 2), "\\%")   # a bound, rounded up to 0.01%
  stopifnot(all(nb$bias > 0), max(abs(nt$bias)) < min(nb$bias))   # the prose: only the bagged mean is biased next year
  cat(sprintf("Next year's payments use only observed histories, and the single-tree fit recovers them: at $n = %s$ its bias is at most %s in absolute value in every scenario, with Monte Carlo standard errors of at most %s. The bagged mean overpredicts them slightly, by %s to %s with Monte Carlo standard errors of at most %s, also where the history carries no information, so this small upward bias of the averaged unpruned trees remains at the largest portfolio; the pruned single tree, fitted without the bootstrap, shows no such bias, and where the history carries no information it is also unbiased for the total (%s to %s across the sizes). ",
              mfmt(nmax), up2(max(abs(nt$bias))), up2(max(nt$bias_se)), pct(min(nb$bias)), pct(max(nb$bias)), pct(max(nb$bias_se)),
              spct(min(none_tree)), spct(max(none_tree))))
  cat(ablation_txt, "")
  grows <- all(diff(vapply(sizes, function(n) b("strong", "tree_plugin", n), 1)) > 0)
  alike <- sign(b("strong", "bag_plugin")) == sign(b("strong", "tree_plugin")) && sign(b("moderate", "bag_plugin")) == sign(b("moderate", "tree_plugin"))
  cat(sprintf("Where the history carries information, the plug-in recursion is biased for the total, and the bias %s: at $n = %s$ the bias of the single-tree fit is %s with strong and %s with moderate persistence, against %s and %s for the plug-in recursion on the true means, which it approaches with strong persistence; %s (%s and %s). With simulated completion, the bias of the total is at most %s in absolute value for both estimators in every scenario at $n = %s$. ",
              if (grows) "grows with the portfolio instead of vanishing" else "does not vanish as the portfolio grows",
              mfmt(nmax), spct(b("strong", "tree_plugin")), spct(b("moderate", "tree_plugin")),
              spct(b("strong", "oracle_plugin")), spct(b("moderate", "oracle_plugin")),
              if (alike) "the bagged mean behaves alike" else "the bagged mean differs",
              spct(b("strong", "bag_plugin")), spct(b("moderate", "bag_plugin")), pct(draw_max), mfmt(nmax)))
  link <- b("strong", "tree_plugin") > 0 && b("moderate", "tree_plugin") > 0 && draw_max < 0.05
  if (link) local({   # the last sentence below: simulated completion does not beat the chain ladder there
    bd <- read.csv(file.path(rev_dir, "bagging_draw.csv")); bdt <- bd[bd$completion == "draw" & bd$component == "total", ]
    bl0 <- read.csv("results/live/table_benchmarks.csv"); mr0 <- read.csv("results/mtpl_rolling/table_rolling.csv")
    stopifnot(abs(bdt$total_mean / bdt$total_truth - 1) > abs(bl0$ultimate_rel_error[bl0$method == "chain ladder"]),
              mean(abs(mr0$total_rel[mr0$method == "bagged mean, simulated completion"])) > mean(abs(mr0$total_rel[mr0$method == "chain ladder"])))
  })
  if (link) cat("The plug-in recursion replaces a claim's uncertain next payment by its mean, a small positive amount that the following trees treat like an observed payment, so that with positive persistence a plugged-in mean crosses the split between paying and non-paying claims and overstates later payments; the same mechanism contributes to the upward bias of the bagged totals in the back-tests above, and a smoother dependence on the history, not examined here, may give a smaller bias. The plug-in default is therefore not a consistent estimator of the multi-year reserve where the occurrence of a payment depends sharply on the history, and for totals we recommend \\code{completion = \"draw\"}, although on the shipped portfolio and the motor extract it does not make the bagged mean more accurate than the chain ladder (\\supp{7}, \\supp{8}).")
  cat("\n")
}
@

\subsection{Replicating this paper} \label{sec:replication}

Every number, table and figure of the article and of its supplementary material is read from the
deposits in \code{results/} of the replication material or computed while knitting; knitting
stops if a file of a deposit differs from its checksum manifest, and an absent deposit is marked
in bold. The \code{Makefile} in \code{paper/} has three levels: \code{make render} knits both
documents from the deposits, computing only the small examples; \code{make replicate} first
recomputes the deposits of the main examples (about half an hour on four cores);
\code{make replicate-all} also recomputes every study summarised in
Section~\ref{sec:evaluation}, which takes hours. The correctly specified simulation of
Section~\ref{sec:well-specified} is not part of it: its full grid takes about 900 core-hours and
was run as a Slurm job array (\code{replication/\allowbreak well\_specified.slurm}), as is its
leaf-size ablation (\code{replication/\allowbreak well\_specified\_ablation.R});
\code{WS\_QUICK=1 make well-specified} and \code{WSA\_QUICK=1 make well-specified-ablation} run
small versions in minutes. The standalone script \code{code.R} runs the displayed code and
recomputes the main examples in a scratch copy, comparing them with the deposits to a relative
tolerance of $10^{-10}$. With \code{CODE\_QUICK=1} it takes minutes and compares only the first
$B = 50$ replicates of the live run and of the motor case with the deposited ones; its
recomputation at $t = 11$ is printed, not compared. The package's vignette
\code{baggingReserve-jss}, this article as a precompiled PDF, is not re-executed by \code{R CMD check}.

%% =============================================================================
\section{Summary and discussion} \label{sec:summary}

<<summary-numbers, echo=FALSE>>=
# the qualitative statements of the first paragraph below
if (ev_ok) stopifnot(all(ev_nine$bag_next < 0), all(ev_nine$bag_total > 0), 2 * nin < n8,
                     mean(rl$draw_total_rel) < mean(rl$bag_total_rel))
oc_share_txt <- if (file.exists("results/open_closed/table_open_closed.csv")) {
  oc <- read.csv("results/open_closed/table_open_closed.csv")
  pct(oc$mean[oc$component == "closed" & oc$horizon == "total"] / oc$mean[oc$component == "all" & oc$horizon == "total"])
} else missing_txt("results/open\\_closed")
@
\pkg{baggingReserve} provides a data contract, a recursion engine with a learner interface and two
completion modes, and back-tests, with the source article's method as the documented default
baseline. On the portfolios studied here the standard chain ladder is the more accurate method on
most of them (Section~\ref{sec:evaluation}); the bagged trees are biased with a consistent sign on
the generator, partly through the plug-in recursion, and good aggregates can rest on offsetting
errors, across accident years, between components and between open and closed claims
(\Sexpr{oc_share_txt} of the reported-claim reserve of the shipped portfolio falls on claims
already closed). On a correctly specified generator the plug-in completion is biased for the total
where the occurrence of a payment depends on the payment history, and simulated completion, which
we recommend for totals, removes most of the bias.

The limitations are those of the design. Only static features enter the panel, so neither a
settlement status that changes over time nor case reserves can be used, and the package cannot
allocate the reserve between open and closed claims; a settlement layer as in \pkg{hirem} would
be needed. The IBNR component holds the projected counts fixed and pools the payments of the
reported claims, which fails where late-reported claims differ in kind; an external count
projection, from \pkg{ReSurv} say, has to be combined with it outside the package. The trees
minimise squared error, which gives large payments a strong influence. The package delivers no
predictive distribution or risk measure of the reserve (Section~\ref{sec:bagging}). The evaluation is limited as well: the
claim-level estimators are compared with the plain chain ladder only, not with a stronger
aggregate method such as the double chain ladder or a bootstrap chain ladder, \pkg{hirem} only on
the open claims, and a tuned configuration of the trees was not examined.

A claim-level method can be expected to do better where the business mix changes, the features
carry real signal, reported claims carry most of the liability and the late development years have
enough paying claims; these are expectations, not results of this paper (the one test of a
changing mix, \supp{6}, was mixed), and \fct{backtest} at several valuation dates checks them.

%% =============================================================================
\section*{Computational details}

The results in this paper were obtained using \proglang{R}~\Sexpr{paste(R.Version()[6:7], collapse = ".")}
\citep{R} with \pkg{baggingReserve}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("baggingReserve")} and
\pkg{rpart}~\Sexpr{packageVersion("rpart")}. The package datasets were generated once with
\pkg{SynthETIC}~\Sexpr{sub("SynthETIC ", "", grep("SynthETIC", generation, value = TRUE))}
\citep{Avanzi+Taylor+Wang+Wong:2021, SynthETIC} and the bundled copy of the generator of
\citet{Wang+Wuthrich:2022}, with its commit identifier recorded in
\code{data-raw/GENERATION.txt} of the replication material. The paper is knitted with \pkg{knitr} \citep{knitr, Xie:2015},
parallel execution uses the \pkg{parallel} package of \proglang{R}, and the package tests use
\pkg{testthat} \citep{Wickham:2011}.
<<reproduction, echo=FALSE, results="asis">>=
repro_dir <- "results/reproduction"
# the package versions recorded by the deposits' sessionInfo files
si <- c("results/sessionInfo.txt", Sys.glob("results/*/sessionInfo*.txt"))
si_txt <- unlist(lapply(si, readLines))
si_v <- sort(unique(sub("baggingReserve_", "", unlist(regmatches(si_txt, gregexpr("baggingReserve_[0-9][0-9.-]*", si_txt))))))
this_v <- as.character(packageVersion("baggingReserve"))
platform_file <- file.path(repro_dir, "platform.csv")
if (file.exists(platform_file)) {
  # TODO(reproduction): adapt the keys to the file written tonight; expected key/value rows
  # deposit_platform, rerun_platform, compared, values, max_rel_diff, identical
  pl <- read.csv(platform_file, stringsAsFactors = FALSE); plv <- setNames(pl$value, pl$key)
  if (length(si_v) != 1L || si_v != this_v)
    cat(sprintf("\\textbf{[the deposits record the package versions %s, but the paper is knitted with %s]}\n", paste(si_v, collapse = ", "), this_v))
  need <- c("deposit_platform", "rerun_platform", "compared", "values", "max_rel_diff", "identical")
  if (!all(need %in% names(plv))) {
    cat("\\textbf{[results/reproduction/platform.csv lacks the keys", paste(gsub("_", "\\\\_", setdiff(need, names(plv))), collapse = ", "), "]}\n")
  } else {
    sci <- function(x) { e <- floor(log10(x)); sprintf("$%s \\times 10^{%d}$", formatC(x / 10^e, format = "f", digits = 1), e) }
    cat(sprintf("All deposited results were computed with \\pkg{baggingReserve}~%s on %s. To check portability, %s were rerun on %s; %s. Earlier deposits computed on macOS agree with these to a relative difference of order $10^{-12}$ (\\code{results/\\allowbreak reproduction/}).\n",
      this_v, gsub("_", "\\\\_", plv[["deposit_platform"]]), gsub("_", "\\\\_", plv[["compared"]]), gsub("_", "\\\\_", plv[["rerun_platform"]]),
      if (as.logical(plv[["identical"]])) sprintf("all %s compared values are identical", fmt(as.numeric(plv[["values"]])))
      else sprintf("the %s compared values agree to a relative difference of at most %s, the level of floating-point rounding", fmt(as.numeric(plv[["values"]])), sci(as.numeric(plv[["max_rel_diff"]])))))
  }
} else if (file.exists(file.path(repro_dir, "record.csv"))) {
  # Until the deposits are recomputed with one version (results/reproduction/platform.csv), the
  # record of the earlier reproduction check is reported.
  rec <- read.csv(file.path(repro_dir, "record.csv"), stringsAsFactors = FALSE); rec <- setNames(rec$value, rec$key)
  cat(sprintf("\\textbf{[Version history until the deposits are recomputed with one version:]} the deposits were computed with the development versions %s; rerunning the scripts of the main deposits with version %s reproduced all %s values of the %s deposited tables and objects that were recomputed (script \\code{replication/\\allowbreak compare\\_deposits.R}).\n",
    paste(si_v, collapse = ", "), rec[["rerun_version"]], fmt(as.numeric(rec[["values"]])), rec[["files"]]))
}
